  // BEGIN BRAIN ATLAS STRUCTURE PROFILES
  var BRAIN_ATLAS_STRUCTURE_PROFILES = {
  "Allen_angular_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The angular gyrus combines information across language, memory, vision, and spatial context. Its left-sided regions often participate in understanding meaning and retrieving learned knowledge, while bilateral regions support remembering events and interpreting situations. Anterior and posterior subdivisions behave differently, so the entire gyrus should not be treated as one calculation or language center.",
    "connectionsText": "Short association fibers link neighboring supramarginal and posterior temporal cortex. Longer pathways, including components of the superior longitudinal and arcuate systems, connect inferior parietal cortex with frontal and temporal regions. Posterior angular regions show particularly strong functional coupling with posterior cingulate, medial prefrontal cortex, and hippocampal memory networks; functional coupling does not establish a direct axon connection.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant-hemisphere parietal injury can disrupt reading, calculation, writing, or knowledge of finger and body relationships, but these difficulties often reflect damage beyond the angular gyrus and its connecting fibers. Right-sided lesions may contribute to spatial attention problems. A person's actual pattern depends on lesion extent and language dominance, rather than the atlas label alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-angular-network",
      "cx-parietal-tracts",
      "cx-angular-functions"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_intermediate_orbital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "This anterior orbital parcel sits within cortex that represents expected outcomes and their current value. Anterior orbitofrontal regions are often recruited when choices involve learned or abstract rewards, although the intermediate gyrus is a surface landmark rather than an independently established decision module. Its contribution is best understood through the surrounding orbital network.",
    "connectionsText": "Neighboring medial and lateral orbital cortex provide local integration, while broader orbitofrontal circuits interact with anterior temporal cortex, amygdala, anterior cingulate, and striatum. The uncinate fasciculus supplies one frontotemporal route. Available connectivity studies usually define larger functional parcels, so these network relationships should not be read as a complete tract map specific to this small gyrus.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage involving anterior orbital networks can make choices less sensitive to changing consequences or surrounding context. Such effects may be subtle on routine language or strength testing. There is no established syndrome unique to the anterior intermediate orbital gyrus; interpretation should consider adjacent orbital cortex, connecting white matter, and the broader consequences of frontal injury.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ofc-gradient",
      "cx-ofc-anticipation",
      "cx-ofc-flexibility",
      "cx-uncinate-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_parahippocampal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The anterior parahippocampal gyrus contains or borders important entorhinal and related medial temporal territories. These regions help connect what is being experienced with stored representations, supporting recognition and the formation of episodic memories. The surface gyrus crosses microscopic boundaries, so its role should be distinguished from a claim that every part performs the same memory operation.",
    "connectionsText": "Entorhinal cortex links surrounding perirhinal and parahippocampal association cortex with the hippocampal formation, including dentate gyrus, hippocampal fields, and subiculum. Human imaging also associates anterior medial temporal regions with lateral temporal and temporopolar cortex. These routes allow object-related, contextual, and emotional information to participate in memory rather than arriving as an isolated sensory stream.",
    "clinicalText": "Anterior medial temporal disease can affect learning and recognition, and this region may participate in temporal lobe seizure networks. Entorhinal involvement is relevant to Alzheimer pathology, but a visible atlas parcel is not itself a diagnostic marker. Verbal versus nonverbal difficulties depend partly on hemisphere, individual organization, and accompanying hippocampal or lateral temporal damage.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-entorhinal",
      "cx-mtl-connectivity",
      "cx-mtl-networks",
      "cx-mtl-memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cingulate_gyrus_caudal_posterior_part": {
    "functionText": "Caudal posterior cingulate cortex helps coordinate internally directed thought, remembered contexts, and changes in attentional focus. It is a major participant in the default mode network, yet its subdivisions also interact with externally directed control systems. Activity here should therefore not be interpreted simply as a brain region becoming inactive whenever a task begins.",
    "connectionsText": "The cingulum links posterior medial cortex with other cingulate and medial temporal territories. Posterior cingulate also shows strong functional relationships with precuneus, angular gyrus, medial prefrontal cortex, and parahippocampal memory networks. These distributed interactions connect remembered scenes and situations with present goals; nearby retrosplenial cortex is related anatomically but is not interchangeable with this whole parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Posteromedial network disruption is associated with memory and orientation difficulties, and altered posterior cingulate activity is frequently studied in Alzheimer disease. These are network-level observations rather than a unique syndrome or diagnostic test for this gyrus. Focal symptoms depend on whether injury extends into retrosplenial cortex, cingulum fibers, or neighboring visual and parietal regions.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-pcc-network",
      "cx-posteromedial-ad",
      "cx-mtl-networks"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cingulate_gyrus_rostral_anterior_part": {
    "functionText": "Rostral anterior cingulate cortex relates emotional significance and expected outcomes to bodily state and motivated behavior. Its position near medial prefrontal and orbital cortex favors integration of valuation with affect. It should be distinguished from more dorsal cingulate regions commonly emphasized in action monitoring, although these functions interact across continuous cortical gradients.",
    "connectionsText": "Pregenual anterior cingulate has prominent functional relationships with medial orbitofrontal cortex; adjacent anterior cingulate territories connect with lateral orbital and inferior frontal systems. Cingulum pathways link medial cortical regions, while broader limbic circuits involve amygdala, striatum, and autonomic control structures. The exact balance varies across rostral, subcallosal, and supracallosal subdivisions rather than following one uniform wiring pattern.",
    "clinicalText": "Changes in anterior cingulate networks are reported in depression and disorders of motivation, while larger medial frontal lesions can reduce spontaneous initiation. A mood symptom cannot be localized to this parcel from an atlas alone. Findings from resting-state imaging describe group associations, and clinical interpretation must account for neighboring cortex, subcortical circuits, and medication effects.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-acc-depression",
      "cx-acc-autonomic",
      "cx-ofc-connectivity",
      "cx-aca-stroke"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cuneus": {
    "functionText": "The cuneus is the medial occipital wedge above the calcarine sulcus. Its calcarine border includes early visual cortex representing predominantly the opposite lower visual field, while surrounding cortex participates in further visual analysis. Visual field maps cross gyral boundaries, so the anatomical cuneus is broader than a single primary or extrastriate visual area.",
    "connectionsText": "Early visual cortex receives geniculocalcarine input from the lateral geniculate nucleus through optic radiations. Local connections link adjacent visual maps, and dorsal extrastriate pathways continue toward parietal regions involved in spatial attention and visually guided action. Connections across the corpus callosum help coordinate the hemispheres, particularly around representations near the vertical visual-field boundary.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage to the calcarine portion can produce a blind region in the opposite lower visual field; larger occipital lesions may cause a more extensive homonymous field defect. The pattern follows retinotopic location and lesion extent rather than the gyrus name alone. Preserved visual responses outside damaged primary cortex do not necessarily imply normal conscious sight.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-retinotopy",
      "cx-visual-maps",
      "cx-visual-lesion"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_frontal_agranular_insular_cortex_area_Fl": {
    "functionText": "This frontal agranular insular parcel belongs to the transition between anterior insula and neighboring frontal limbic cortex. The surrounding system integrates bodily feelings, emotional relevance, and behaviorally important events. Agranular describes its cellular organization, not a single mental faculty; current human functional maps do not establish a unique computation for this exact atlas boundary.",
    "connectionsText": "Anterior insular subdivisions have relationships with anterior cingulate, orbitofrontal, ventrolateral prefrontal, and limbic regions. Local fibers connect adjacent insular and opercular cortex, while broader association pathways link frontal and temporal systems. These connections support coordination between visceral or affective information and action, but subdivision-level tractography should not be mistaken for a definitive wiring diagram of this microscopic parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury involving anterior insular networks may alter bodily awareness, emotional experience, or coordination of responses to salient events. Seizures can produce visceral or autonomic sensations, but their apparent symptoms may reflect rapid spread. No specific syndrome is established for this frontal agranular parcel alone, and nearby opercular and deep white-matter involvement can change the presentation substantially.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-insula-tracts",
      "cx-insula-gradient",
      "cx-insula-stimulation",
      "cx-insula-anatomy",
      "cx-allen-atlas"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_frontal_operculum": {
    "functionText": "The frontal operculum forms part of the cortical cover over the insula and participates in coordinating oral actions, speech, and task demands. Dominant-hemisphere portions contribute to language production, while surrounding opercular networks also support nonspeech functions. The label spans anatomical territory and should not be used as a synonym for Broca's area or a single speech center.",
    "connectionsText": "Local connections join inferior frontal, ventral precentral, and anterior insular cortex. Dorsal language pathways connect frontal articulatory systems with posterior temporal and inferior parietal regions, and medial frontal links support initiation and sequencing. These interactions connect linguistic plans with motor execution; which fibers and cortical sites are essential varies between individuals and across the opercular extent.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant frontal opercular lesions can cause speech or language production problems, especially when ventral motor cortex or connecting fibers are involved. Bilateral opercular injury may impair voluntary facial, tongue, and swallowing movements. Surgical studies emphasize individual mapping: neither normal conversation nor the anatomical label alone reliably defines the functional limits of a lesion or resection.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-opercular-surgery",
      "cx-ifg-syntax",
      "cx-broca-network",
      "cx-sensorimotor-stimulation"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_frontal_pole": {
    "functionText": "The frontal pole helps organize behavior beyond the immediate task, including maintaining future intentions, comparing alternative courses of action, and coordinating multiple goals. Medial and lateral portions participate in different networks. Its contribution is especially relevant when a person must remember to act later while continuing another activity, rather than merely retaining an item briefly.",
    "connectionsText": "Rostral prefrontal regions interact with dorsolateral frontal and inferior parietal control networks, while medial portions relate more closely to medial prefrontal and default mode systems. Frontotemporal connections include routes through the uncinate fasciculus. This mixture supports integrating current plans with remembered and socially relevant information; the entire frontal pole does not share an identical connectivity profile.",
    "clinicalText": "Rostral prefrontal lesions may impair prospective memory or management of competing goals despite relatively preserved basic language and intelligence measures. Difficulties can appear as missed intentions or inefficient everyday organization. Lesion studies support a role for this region, but laterality, task demands, and involvement of adjacent frontal cortex prevent assigning one universal frontal-pole syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-frontopolar-lesion",
      "cx-frontoparietal-control",
      "cx-uncinate-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_frontomarginal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The frontomarginal gyrus is a small surface feature near the anterior frontal margin, where frontopolar and orbital territories meet. It is most usefully placed within networks for considering future goals and evaluating possible outcomes. Its boundaries do not define a validated, independent cognitive module, so no unique psychological function should be assigned to the gyrus itself.",
    "connectionsText": "Nearby frontopolar, orbital, and lateral prefrontal cortex provide the immediate anatomical context. Larger rostral frontal networks interact with parietal control regions and anterior temporal cortex, including frontotemporal association routes. Direct human evidence specific to the frontomarginal gyrus is limited; named network partners here describe its surrounding system rather than proven connections for every part of this parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "A lesion centered near this gyrus should be assessed for effects on goal management and value-guided behavior, alongside injury to neighboring cortex and white matter. There is no established frontomarginal-gyrus syndrome or reliable single bedside test. Apparent sparing of elementary motor or language abilities would not exclude difficulties in complex daily planning or social decision making.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-frontopolar-lesion",
      "cx-ofc-flexibility",
      "cx-frontoparietal-control"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_gyrus_ambiens": {
    "functionText": "The gyrus ambiens is a medial temporal prominence closely associated with the medial intermediate entorhinal subfield. It therefore belongs to a cortical interface involved in memory and spatial-context processing. Histological work clarifies this relationship, but does not establish a unique behavioral function for the prominence or justify treating it as a separate memory center.",
    "connectionsText": "Its entorhinal setting places it within interactions between perirhinal and parahippocampal association cortex and the hippocampal formation. Entorhinal pathways participate in hippocampal input and output, with different subregions showing different relationships to subiculum and surrounding cortex. Those broader connections are better established than a complete human tract map restricted specifically to the visible gyrus ambiens.",
    "clinicalText": "This landmark matters when interpreting medial temporal imaging and planning approaches near the uncus. Entorhinal tissue is relevant to memory disorders, including Alzheimer disease, and the surrounding region participates in temporal seizure networks. Neither atrophy nor a lesion here predicts a unique gyrus-ambiens syndrome; neighboring hippocampal, amygdalar, and entorhinal involvement must be considered.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ambiens",
      "cx-entorhinal",
      "cx-mtl-memory",
      "cx-piriform-epilepsy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_gyrus_rectus_straight_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The gyrus rectus occupies the medial orbital surface beside the olfactory sulcus. It forms part of a broader ventromedial frontal environment concerned with value, emotion, and behavior in context. Its convenient surgical landmark status does not make it functionless, but evidence also does not support attributing one exclusive decision or personality function to this gyrus.",
    "connectionsText": "Neighboring medial orbital and subcallosal regions link this territory to wider ventromedial prefrontal networks. These systems interact with anterior cingulate, amygdala, anterior temporal cortex, and ventral striatal circuits; frontotemporal routes include the uncinate fasciculus. The nearby olfactory tract is a separate structure, and proximity alone does not show that every part of the gyrus directly receives olfactory-bulb input.",
    "clinicalText": "The gyrus rectus is encountered during anterior communicating artery aneurysm surgery. Small surgical series have examined cognitive outcomes after limited resection, but results are confounded by hemorrhage and other injury and cannot establish that the tissue is dispensable. Memory, behavioral, or olfactory changes require assessment of adjacent basal forebrain, vessels, olfactory structures, and frontal connections.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-rectus-surgery",
      "cx-ofc-medial-lateral",
      "cx-ofc-networks",
      "cx-uncinate-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_frontal_gyrus_opercular_part": {
    "functionText": "The opercular inferior frontal gyrus contributes to organizing speech-related sequences and grammatical production, particularly in the language-dominant hemisphere. It also participates in broader action-control networks, with functions differing across hemisphere and subregion. Anatomical overlap with classical Broca territory is useful for orientation, but speech production emerges from a connected frontal, temporal, parietal, and motor system.",
    "connectionsText": "Local fibers link the opercular part with ventral premotor cortex, anterior insula, and neighboring triangular inferior frontal cortex. Arcuate and superior longitudinal pathways connect frontal language-related regions with posterior temporal and inferior parietal cortex. Connections with medial frontal systems contribute to initiation and sequencing; the tract endpoint and functional role should be assessed at finer resolution than the gyrus alone.",
    "clinicalText": "Direct stimulation can interfere with sentence production at some dominant inferior frontal sites. Persistent nonfluent aphasia, however, usually cannot be explained by injury to this cortical label alone and often involves deeper pathways or other regions. Right-sided involvement may affect action control or speech prosody, with the exact pattern depending on individual organization and lesion extent.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ifg-syntax",
      "cx-broca-network",
      "cx-parietal-tracts",
      "cx-ofc-connectivity"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_frontal_gyrus_triangular_part": {
    "functionText": "The triangular inferior frontal gyrus helps select and organize relevant linguistic information. In the dominant hemisphere, it is recruited when meaning must be retrieved under competition and when sentences are assembled. Its functions overlap with neighboring frontal language regions, but the emphasis on controlled retrieval is useful when distinguishing it from a purely articulatory motor area.",
    "connectionsText": "This region interacts with posterior middle temporal cortex and other temporal semantic regions, as well as neighboring opercular and orbital inferior frontal cortex. Dorsal and ventral frontotemporal routes support different aspects of language processing. Functional studies identify a distributed semantic-control system, so synchronized activity between frontal and temporal sites should not automatically be labeled a single direct anatomical tract.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant-hemisphere injury can make word selection or context-appropriate semantic retrieval difficult, and stimulation at some sites disrupts sentence construction. These findings do not mean that every triangular-gyrus lesion causes the same aphasia. Performance depends on the affected connections, language dominance, and the demands of the task, including how strongly competing interpretations must be suppressed.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-semantic-control",
      "cx-ifg-syntax",
      "cx-semantic-network",
      "cx-broca-network"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_occipital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The inferior occipital gyrus participates in extrastriate visual analysis, including features that contribute to recognizing objects and faces. A face-selective region is often found in right inferior occipital cortex, but occupies only part of this anatomical territory. Other portions contribute to broader visual representations, and individual functional boundaries vary relative to the visible folds.",
    "connectionsText": "Local visual pathways exchange information with early occipital cortex and ventral occipitotemporal regions, including fusiform cortex. Longer occipitotemporal association routes, notably the inferior longitudinal fasciculus, link posterior visual information with anterior temporal systems. Face-related processing involves a distributed network that also includes superior temporal regions; the whole gyrus should not be reduced to a single feedforward face pathway.",
    "clinicalText": "Stimulation of a right face-selective inferior occipital site has disrupted discrimination between faces, providing causal evidence for that localized function. Larger lesions may affect object recognition or combine recognition problems with visual-field loss. Prosopagnosia is a network disorder with several possible lesion sites, so it should not be predicted from this parcel's involvement alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-occipital-face",
      "cx-ilf-anatomy",
      "cx-ventral-maps"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_temporal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "Inferior temporal cortex helps turn complex visual patterns into representations useful for recognizing objects and accessing their meaning. Posterior portions are closer to visual analysis, while more anterior regions interact strongly with conceptual and memory systems. The gyrus contains multiple functional territories rather than one universal object detector, and left-right differences depend on stimulus and task.",
    "connectionsText": "The inferior longitudinal fasciculus links occipital and posterior ventral temporal regions with anterior temporal cortex. Local connections join fusiform and middle temporal cortex, while medial temporal relationships help connect recognized items with familiarity and memory. Frontal interactions support naming and selection, although visual recognition and lexical retrieval need not be disrupted together by the same lesion.",
    "clinicalText": "Ventral temporal injury can impair recognition or naming of visually presented objects. Left-sided lesions and disruption of the inferior longitudinal fasciculus may particularly affect picture naming, while right-sided ventral temporal damage can contribute to nonverbal recognition problems. The deficit depends on whether visual representations, semantic knowledge, or access to words has been interrupted, requiring separate assessment.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ilf-anatomy",
      "cx-ilf-naming",
      "cx-mtl-transition",
      "cx-semantic-network"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_occipitotemporal_fusiform_gyrus_occipital_part": {
    "functionText": "The occipital portion of the fusiform gyrus participates in ventral visual processing between early occipital maps and more anterior temporal representations. It helps analyze structured visual patterns used in recognizing faces, objects, and written forms. Functional selectivity varies across small neighboring patches, so this anatomical segment is not identical to either the face area or the word-form area.",
    "connectionsText": "Local pathways connect this posterior fusiform territory with inferior occipital, lingual, and temporal fusiform cortex. The inferior longitudinal fasciculus provides a longer route toward anterior temporal regions. These connections allow visual features to influence recognition and meaning, while interactions with language networks become especially relevant for reading in the dominant hemisphere.",
    "clinicalText": "Left posterior occipitotemporal lesions can cause slow, effortful reading or pure alexia, especially when word-selective cortex or its connections are affected. Right-sided ventral lesions may contribute to face-recognition difficulties. Neither syndrome maps perfectly onto the atlas's occipital fusiform boundary; visual-field loss, white-matter disconnection, and extension into adjacent cortex can substantially alter the presentation.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ventral-maps",
      "cx-alexia-lesion",
      "cx-ilf-anatomy",
      "cx-fusiform-stimulation"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_olfactory_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "This small basal frontal landmark belongs to the anatomical neighborhood of the lateral olfactory pathway and primary olfactory-related cortex. The surrounding network transforms odor information into recognizable and behaviorally relevant experiences. The gyral name alone does not establish a separate olfactory operation, and its surface boundaries should not be equated automatically with all piriform cortex.",
    "connectionsText": "The lateral olfactory stria carries bulb output toward primary olfactory territories, including piriform and amygdalar regions. Those systems interact with entorhinal and orbitofrontal cortex, linking odors with memory and value. These are relationships of the surrounding olfactory network; evidence does not justify assigning every named projection specifically to the entire small lateral olfactory gyrus parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Basal frontal injury in this neighborhood can accompany altered smell, but damage to the bulb, tract, nasal input, or adjacent olfactory cortex may be responsible. Olfactory auras can occur in temporal seizure networks and do not uniquely localize here. There is no established clinical syndrome specific to the lateral olfactory gyrus as an isolated anatomical unit.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-olfactory-network",
      "cx-piriform-epilepsy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_orbital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "Lateral orbitofrontal cortex helps link particular choices with their consequences and update behavior when expected outcomes change. Human lesion evidence supports a contribution to assigning credit to the action that actually produced a reward. This is more precise than labeling the region a punishment center, since learning depends on both outcomes and the context in which they occur.",
    "connectionsText": "Lateral orbital regions interact with inferior frontal cortex, anterior cingulate, temporal association cortex, amygdala, and striatal circuits. Compared with medial orbital regions, their functional connectivity more strongly involves lateral cognitive and action-related networks. Frontotemporal association fibers and local orbital connections provide anatomical routes, but connectivity differs across the anterior-posterior extent of the lateral orbital gyrus.",
    "clinicalText": "Lateral orbital lesions can weaken learning from the relationship between a particular choice and its outcome, leading to poorly adjusted decisions even when rewards are noticed. Broader orbital injury may also affect social behavior or flexibility. These patterns are variable and should not be treated as a personality diagnosis or a fixed consequence of unilateral gyrus involvement.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ofc-lesions",
      "cx-ofc-medial-lateral",
      "cx-ofc-connectivity",
      "cx-ofc-flexibility"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_limen_insula": {
    "functionText": "The limen insula is the threshold where the insular surface meets the basal frontotemporal region. Its major value is as an anatomical junction rather than a separately demonstrated cognitive center. Surrounding anterior insular and temporal-orbital systems integrate bodily state, affect, and meaning, but those broader functions cannot be assigned exclusively to this narrow cortical ridge.",
    "connectionsText": "The uncinate fasciculus curves beneath this region, connecting anterior temporal and amygdalar territory with orbital and rostral frontal cortex. Nearby temporal-stem fibers include other frontotemporal and fronto-occipital pathways. These are important deep relationships, not proof that passing axons terminate in the limen itself; distinguishing cortex from underlying bundles is essential when interpreting a highlighted atlas structure.",
    "clinicalText": "The limen is a useful surgical landmark because lesions or approaches near it can affect frontotemporal fibers and adjacent vascular structures. Consequences may include language, memory, or behavioral changes depending on what is actually injured. No characteristic limen-insula syndrome is established, and a small surface lesion must be distinguished from deeper temporal-stem disconnection.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-temporal-stem",
      "cx-uncinate-anatomy",
      "cx-insula-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lingual_gyrus_medial_occipitotemporal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The lingual gyrus lies below the calcarine sulcus and includes early and ventral visual territories. Its calcarine portion represents predominantly the opposite upper visual field, while more ventral regions participate in visual-form and scene-related processing. The gyrus extends across several functional maps, making it broader than either primary visual cortex or a specialized reading area.",
    "connectionsText": "Calcarine cortex receives lateral geniculate input through optic radiations, then exchanges information with neighboring visual maps. Connections with fusiform and parahippocampal regions place more ventral lingual territory within visual-recognition and contextual networks. Occipitotemporal association pathways continue toward temporal cortex, while interhemispheric visual connections help coordinate information across the two sides of the visual field.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions near the lower calcarine bank can cause opposite upper-field loss. Left medial occipital injury may slow reading through a visual-field defect, while pure alexia usually implicates additional ventral occipitotemporal cortex or disconnection. These distinctions matter: difficulty reading after a lingual-region stroke does not by itself demonstrate destruction of a dedicated word-recognition area.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-visual-maps",
      "cx-ventral-maps",
      "cx-alexia-lesion",
      "cx-alexia-disconnection"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_long_insular_gyri": {
    "functionText": "The long insular gyri occupy much of the posterior insula, where processing emphasizes bodily sensation, including pain, temperature, and somatosensory integration. Responses differ across dorsal and ventral locations, and some sites combine multiple aspects of bodily experience. The anatomical gyri provide orientation but do not coincide perfectly with functional subdivisions revealed by stimulation or connectivity studies.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior insular regions interact with somatosensory cortex, parietal operculum, sensorimotor regions, and thalamic pathways. Local insular connections allow sensory information to influence more anterior affective and control-related systems. Structural and functional studies support a posterior-to-anterior gradient, with overlapping connections rather than sharply isolated sensory and emotional compartments throughout the insular cortex.",
    "clinicalText": "Posterior insular stimulation can evoke somatic, painful, or temperature-related sensations. Lesions may alter aspects of bodily sensation, while seizures can produce sensory symptoms that resemble activity in nearby opercular cortex. A symptom alone cannot identify an individual long gyrus, because clinical lesions and electrical effects often include neighboring tissue or spread through connected networks.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-insula-stimulation",
      "cx-insula-gradient",
      "cx-insula-tracts"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_medial_orbital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "Medial orbital cortex participates in comparing the value of available outcomes and placing decisions within personal and emotional context. Human lesion evidence suggests that this system helps keep irrelevant alternatives from distorting choice. It overlaps conceptually with ventromedial prefrontal networks, but the anatomical medial orbital gyrus does not encompass every region involved in value representation.",
    "connectionsText": "Medial orbital regions interact with pregenual anterior cingulate, medial prefrontal and posterior default mode regions, as well as amygdala, hippocampal systems, and striatum. Olfactory information can also converge on orbitofrontal networks through piriform and related pathways. These connections combine remembered and sensory consequences with present value, with differing patterns across the orbital surface.",
    "clinicalText": "Medial orbital or ventromedial frontal lesions may produce decisions that are unusually influenced by distracting alternatives or poorly aligned with longer-term consequences. Emotional and social difficulties can accompany larger lesions. The effect is not equivalent to loss of all reasoning, and no single behavioral score or anatomical parcel can establish a specific psychiatric condition.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ofc-lesions",
      "cx-ofc-medial-lateral",
      "cx-ofc-connectivity",
      "cx-olfactory-network"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_middle_frontal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The middle frontal gyrus contains substantial lateral prefrontal territory involved in maintaining goals, manipulating working information, and adjusting attention or strategies. Posterior regions lie closer to premotor and eye-movement systems, whereas more anterior regions support increasingly abstract control. This long gyrus contains multiple network nodes and should not be portrayed as one uniformly functioning executive center.",
    "connectionsText": "Lateral frontal control regions interact with inferior and superior parietal cortex, neighboring superior and inferior frontal regions, and temporal association areas. Frontal-subcortical loops also involve striatum and thalamus. Functional studies distinguish control-network components that couple more strongly with default mode versus dorsal attention systems, helping explain why different portions support internal planning and externally directed task control.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury can cause distractibility, difficulty holding or manipulating task information, and inefficient switching between rules. These deficits may emerge most clearly during complex activities despite preserved elementary sensation and movement. Left-right differences vary with verbal or spatial demands, and lesion location within the gyrus and associated white-matter damage are more informative than the broad label alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-frontoparietal-control",
      "cx-sfg-connectivity",
      "cx-sfg-lesion"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_middle_temporal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The middle temporal gyrus contributes to understanding words, events, and conceptual relationships, with different roles along its length. Left posterior regions help retrieve meaning appropriate to the current context, especially when automatic associations are insufficient. More anterior portions interact with broader conceptual systems, while posterior lateral temporal territories also participate in visual and multimodal processing.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior middle temporal cortex interacts with inferior frontal semantic-control regions and inferior parietal cortex; more anterior portions connect with temporal-pole and medial temporal systems. Tractography reveals distinct connectivity-defined subdivisions rather than one uniform temporal strip. These pathways allow perceptual representations, stored knowledge, and current task requirements to influence one another during comprehension and retrieval.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant posterior temporal injury may impair comprehension or the controlled retrieval of meaning, while more anterior temporal disease can degrade conceptual knowledge itself. These are different mechanisms and may require different testing. Aphasia patterns depend on adjacent superior temporal cortex and connecting white matter, so damage to the middle temporal gyrus does not predict one fixed language syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-semantic-control",
      "cx-mtg-parcellation",
      "cx-semantic-network",
      "cx-temporal-pole-degeneration"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_occipital_pole": {
    "functionText": "The occipital pole contains posterior visual cortex where the central visual field has a disproportionately large representation. It is especially important for the fine visual information used when inspecting an object or reading. Primary and neighboring extrastriate maps meet in this region, and the visible pole does not mark one sharply bounded functional area.",
    "connectionsText": "Geniculocalcarine fibers bring visual signals from the lateral geniculate nucleus to primary visual cortex. Dense local connections distribute this information through adjacent occipital maps, with downstream dorsal parietal and ventral temporal pathways supporting action and recognition. The two hemispheres communicate through posterior callosal connections, particularly around visual representations close to the vertical meridian.",
    "clinicalText": "A lesion involving central-field representations can impair vision near fixation, making reading or detailed inspection difficult even when peripheral vision is better preserved. Other occipital lesions may spare the central field. The precise defect must be measured with visual-field testing because cortical magnification, lesion size, and vascular variation prevent a simple one-to-one prediction from the atlas surface.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-retinotopy",
      "cx-visual-maps",
      "cx-visual-lesion",
      "cx-alexia-lesion"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_occipitotemporal_fusiform_gyrus_temporal_part": {
    "functionText": "Temporal fusiform cortex supports detailed visual representations used to identify faces, objects, and familiar written patterns. Selective patches differ across the gyrus and between hemispheres; right-sided face processing has particularly strong causal evidence from stimulation. The temporal fusiform label spans more than a single face area, and visual familiarity should be distinguished from retrieving a person's identity or name.",
    "connectionsText": "Local links connect posterior fusiform and inferior occipital visual regions with more anterior fusiform and inferior temporal cortex. The inferior longitudinal fasciculus carries occipitotemporal associations toward anterior temporal systems, while medial temporal interactions link perception with familiarity. These relationships support recognition across a network, so preserved activity in one face-selective patch does not guarantee successful recognition.",
    "clinicalText": "Right fusiform stimulation can distort face perception or temporarily disrupt face recognition at particular sites. Left ventral occipitotemporal injury can interfere with rapid reading, while broader lesions may cause visual agnosia. Clinical effects depend on the affected patch and connections; a lesion anywhere in the temporal fusiform gyrus should not automatically be labeled prosopagnosia or pure alexia.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-fusiform-stimulation",
      "cx-fusiform-anterior",
      "cx-ilf-anatomy",
      "cx-alexia-disconnection"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_paracentral_lobule_caudal_part": {
    "functionText": "The caudal paracentral lobule continues the postcentral sensory territory onto the medial hemisphere and represents bodily information particularly from the opposite lower limb. This includes signals needed to judge limb position and contact with the environment. Its boundary with adjacent motor and association cortex varies anatomically, so the surface parcel is an approximate guide to the sensory map.",
    "connectionsText": "Somatosensory thalamic input reaches medial postcentral territories through ascending thalamocortical pathways. Local connections join the rostral paracentral motor region and lateral postcentral gyrus, while parietal association and opercular circuits support interpretation of bodily information. These relationships allow lower-limb sensation to contribute to movement and body representation rather than remaining isolated from motor planning.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury may reduce sensation or position awareness in the opposite leg, sometimes accompanying weakness when adjacent motor territory is affected. Anterior cerebral artery strokes can involve this medial region, but their broader symptoms reflect the full vascular territory. Bladder or motivational changes should not be attributed specifically to the caudal sensory parcel without considering other medial frontal structures.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-paracentral-anatomy",
      "cx-sensorimotor-stimulation",
      "cx-somatosensory-attention",
      "cx-aca-stroke"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_paracentral_lobule_rostral_part": {
    "functionText": "The rostral paracentral lobule continues precentral motor territory onto the medial surface, with a strong role in controlling the opposite leg and foot. It works with supplementary and premotor systems to implement voluntary actions. The familiar motor map is an orientation aid rather than a collection of perfectly separate body-part compartments within this gyrus.",
    "connectionsText": "Descending corticospinal pathways carry motor output toward spinal circuits, while local connections exchange information with the neighboring sensory paracentral region. Supplementary motor, premotor, and cingulate motor systems contribute to action organization, and thalamocortical loops convey subcortical influences. These pathways place lower-limb control within a distributed network for planned movement, posture, and sensory feedback.",
    "clinicalText": "A lesion involving medial motor cortex can produce opposite leg-predominant weakness, as often occurs in anterior cerebral artery infarction. Larger medial frontal lesions may add reduced initiation or continence problems, but those are not exclusive functions of this motor parcel. The severity of weakness also depends on injury to descending fibers and the extent of spared motor cortex.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-motor-map",
      "cx-paracentral-anatomy",
      "cx-sensorimotor-stimulation",
      "cx-aca-stroke"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_paracingulate_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The paracingulate gyrus is a variable medial frontal fold beside cingulate cortex. Nearby medial prefrontal systems contribute to monitoring internally generated information, evaluating actions, and reasoning about oneself or others. Research links paracingulate morphology with aspects of reality monitoring, but the presence or size of a fold does not determine an individual's abilities or define a single functional module.",
    "connectionsText": "The surrounding medial frontal territory interacts with anterior cingulate, superior frontal, and other prefrontal regions. Functional relationships with posterior cingulate and temporoparietal systems vary along its anterior-posterior extent and with task demands. Because the paracingulate fold differs substantially between people, connections defined in standardized functional maps cannot simply be transferred to every anatomically labeled gyrus.",
    "clinicalText": "Paracingulate anatomy has been associated with individual differences in distinguishing remembered events from imagined ones, but these group findings are not diagnostic of hallucinations or psychiatric illness. Lesions in the broader medial frontal network may affect monitoring or initiation. Clinical interpretation should prioritize symptoms, lesion extent, and adjacent cingulate or frontal involvement over normal sulcal variation.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-paracingulate",
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-sfg-connectivity",
      "cx-frontoparietal-control"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_parietal_operculum": {
    "functionText": "The parietal operculum contains several secondary somatosensory territories that analyze tactile input, including texture, shape, and other discriminations relevant to recognizing objects by touch. Many responses are bilateral and depend on attention or task demands. Its subdivisions are not interchangeable, and the entire operculum should not be portrayed as a single uniform secondary sensory area.",
    "connectionsText": "Opercular somatosensory regions interact with primary somatosensory cortex, posterior insula, inferior and superior parietal cortex, and motor-related areas. Bilateral cortical relationships help integrate information across body regions. These connections support using tactile information for identification and action, while different opercular subdivisions contribute differently to sensory encoding, attention, and higher-order discrimination.",
    "clinicalText": "Opercular injury may impair higher-order tactile judgments or the ability to use sensory feedback effectively, sometimes despite residual detection of simple touch. Nearby insular or sensorimotor injury can add pain, temperature, or movement abnormalities. Small rehabilitation and connectivity studies support a network role after stroke, but do not establish a unique clinical syndrome for every opercular subdivision.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-parietal-operculum",
      "cx-somatosensory-attention",
      "cx-opercular-stroke",
      "cx-insula-tracts"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_perirhinal_gyrus_rostral_part_of_FuGt": {
    "functionText": "This rostral perirhinal territory lies at the interface between complex object representations and medial temporal memory systems. It contributes to recognizing familiar items and distinguishing objects with overlapping features. The atlas places it within a particular rostral fusiform region, but perirhinal microscopic boundaries and functional specializations should not be assumed to follow one visible gyral edge exactly.",
    "connectionsText": "Perirhinal cortex interacts with inferior and anterior temporal association cortex and with entorhinal cortex, which provides a major route into hippocampal circuits. Human connectivity studies associate rostral perirhinal regions especially with an anterior temporal system, while more caudal regions bridge toward posterior medial networks. These relationships link item identity and familiarity with broader episodic context.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage involving perirhinal and adjacent anterior medial temporal cortex can affect recognition and discrimination of complex items. It may also contribute to memory impairment in temporal lobe disease, but isolated human lesions are uncommon. A deficit should not be assigned solely to this rostral parcel without considering entorhinal, hippocampal, and ventral temporal involvement and the specific task used.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-mtl-transition",
      "cx-mtl-connectivity",
      "cx-mtl-memory",
      "cx-entorhinal"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_piriform_region": {
    "functionText": "Piriform cortex is a major primary olfactory territory that combines patterns of odor input into representations useful for distinguishing and recognizing smells. Its recurrent association circuitry supports interactions between current sensory input and prior experience. Unlike vision or audition, initial olfactory cortical input does not require an obligatory thalamic relay before reaching this primary cortical network.",
    "connectionsText": "The olfactory bulb reaches piriform cortex through the lateral olfactory pathway. Piriform regions interact with amygdala, entorhinal cortex, and other olfactory areas; orbitofrontal relationships include direct and thalamically mediated routes within the broader network. These pathways connect odor identity with affect, memory, and value, while recurrent intrinsic connections allow distributed odor patterns to influence one another.",
    "clinicalText": "Piriform cortex is relevant to focal epilepsy because its dense excitatory network can participate in seizure generation and spread. An unpleasant or otherwise unusual smell can be an aura, but does not uniquely identify piriform onset. Olfactory deficits likewise have many peripheral and central causes, so this region's involvement requires interpretation alongside the wider clinical and anatomical picture.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-piriform-epilepsy",
      "cx-olfactory-network"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_planum_polare": {
    "functionText": "The planum polare lies anterior to Heschl's gyrus on the superior temporal plane. It belongs to auditory association territory that helps elaborate sound patterns beyond initial acoustic analysis, including information useful for identifying meaningful sounds. It is part of an anterior auditory processing stream, although its anatomical boundaries do not define a single speech or music function.",
    "connectionsText": "Human imaging supports a route from primary auditory cortex toward lateral planum polare and anterior superior temporal cortex. Local superior temporal connections and more anterior temporal interactions place this region within auditory object and meaning-related networks. This anterior route complements posterior auditory pathways; effective connectivity from imaging estimates information flow but does not independently prove a monosynaptic anatomical connection.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage involving anterior auditory association networks may affect recognition or interpretation of complex sounds, with the pattern depending on hemisphere and neighboring temporal involvement. There is no established isolated planum-polare syndrome. Hearing acuity, auditory discrimination, language comprehension, and nonverbal sound recognition should be distinguished because a problem in one does not imply impairment of all auditory functions.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-auditory-streams",
      "cx-auditory-depth",
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-auditory-lesions"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_planum_temporale": {
    "functionText": "The planum temporale lies behind Heschl's gyrus and contributes to higher-order auditory analysis, including relationships among changing sounds and their spatial or sequential structure. Left-sided regions often participate in speech-related processing, but the area is not exclusively linguistic. Its common anatomical asymmetry does not provide a simple individual test of language dominance.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior auditory pathways link primary auditory cortex with lateral planum temporale and posterior superior temporal regions. Connections with inferior parietal and frontal systems allow sound information to inform attention and speech-related actions. These posterior interactions complement anterior auditory routes toward the planum polare, with substantial local exchange rather than a strictly one-directional chain.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions in posterior superior temporal networks may disturb auditory discrimination, speech comprehension, or sound-to-action processing. The exact deficit depends on extent and dominant-hemisphere organization; severe cortical deafness generally requires more extensive bilateral auditory injury. A planum-temporale abnormality alone should not be equated with Wernicke aphasia or used to explain a developmental language difficulty without further evidence.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-planum-temporale",
      "cx-auditory-streams",
      "cx-auditory-depth",
      "cx-auditory-lesions",
      "cx-cortical-deafness"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_postcentral_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The postcentral gyrus contains major primary somatosensory territories that map touch, limb position, and other bodily information, mainly from the opposite side. Different cortical areas emphasize different features, and representations are weighted toward behaviorally sensitive body parts. This organized sensory map supplies information for perception and skilled movement rather than functioning as a passive display of incoming signals.",
    "connectionsText": "Thalamocortical pathways relay body and facial information from somatosensory thalamic nuclei. Local connections link adjacent primary sensory areas and precentral motor cortex, while pathways toward superior parietal cortex and parietal operculum support more integrated judgments. The medial continuation into the paracentral lobule extends this system toward lower-limb representations and their corresponding motor networks.",
    "clinicalText": "A lesion can cause opposite-sided sensory loss or impaired position sense, with the affected body region following the cortical map. Higher-order deficits such as poor tactile object recognition may involve adjacent association cortex as well. Sensory seizures can produce spreading tingling, but the exact onset site requires more than the subjective order of symptoms to establish.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-sensorimotor-stimulation",
      "cx-somatosensory-attention",
      "cx-parietal-operculum",
      "cx-paracentral-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_posterior_intermediate_orbital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "This posterior orbital parcel lies near cortex that combines sensory and affective information about possible outcomes. Posterior orbitofrontal regions are often more closely associated with concrete sensory rewards than more anterior regions. The intermediate gyrus itself is an anatomical division, however, and available evidence does not support assigning it one exclusive reward modality or learning operation.",
    "connectionsText": "Nearby orbital and agranular insular cortex exchange information with olfactory, gustatory, somatosensory, and temporal-limbic systems. Broader orbital circuits include amygdala, anterior cingulate, and striatal relationships. These connections provide a route from sensory qualities to outcome evaluation, but studies typically resolve larger cortical areas rather than all projections of this particular posterior intermediate gyral parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury involving posterior orbital networks may disturb the use of sensory consequences in guiding behavior, particularly when adjacent insular or temporal connections are also affected. No established syndrome isolates this small gyrus. Changes in smell, taste, reward learning, or social behavior should be evaluated separately rather than assumed to arise together from its anatomical involvement.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-ofc-gradient",
      "cx-ofc-networks",
      "cx-ofc-flexibility",
      "cx-ofc-anticipation"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_posterior_parahippocampal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "Posterior parahippocampal cortex helps represent environmental scenes and the contextual relationships in which events occur. It supports memory by linking an item or action with its surroundings rather than only signaling familiarity. Scene-selective activity occupies part of this territory, but the entire anatomical gyrus has broader contextual and mnemonic roles that vary across its extent.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior parahippocampal regions show strong relationships with retrosplenial and posterior cingulate cortex, medial and lateral parietal regions, and posterior hippocampal systems. Entorhinal pathways provide an important interface with hippocampal circuitry. These connections support integrating visual environments with remembered spatial context, contrasting with the more anterior temporal emphasis of rostral perirhinal networks without forming an absolute division.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage to posterior medial temporal networks can impair learning or recognizing environmental context and may contribute to navigation difficulties. Broader medial temporal disease can also affect episodic memory. Such symptoms do not uniquely localize to this gyrus: visual, retrosplenial, hippocampal, and connecting white-matter involvement must be considered, as well as whether the task tests perception or memory.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-mtl-connectivity",
      "cx-mtl-networks",
      "cx-mtl-transition",
      "cx-mtl-memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_precentral_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The precentral gyrus contains primary motor and adjacent motor-related cortex that contributes to voluntary movement, especially skilled control of the opposite side of the body. Face and hand representations occupy substantial lateral territory, while lower-limb control extends medially. Motor representations overlap and participate in coordinated actions rather than acting as isolated switches for individual muscles.",
    "connectionsText": "Corticospinal and corticobulbar pathways carry descending motor signals toward spinal and brainstem circuits. Inputs from premotor and supplementary motor regions help organize actions, while postcentral sensory cortex supplies feedback. Thalamic inputs convey influences from cerebellar and basal ganglia loops, allowing motor output to reflect learned plans, current body state, and ongoing corrections.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage can cause opposite-sided weakness with a distribution related to the cortical body map, while deeper descending-pathway injury may produce a broader deficit. Focal motor seizures can cause rhythmic movements that spread across body regions. Strength, dexterity, speech articulation, and associated sensory findings help distinguish cortical injury from other causes of impaired movement.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-motor-map",
      "cx-sensorimotor-stimulation",
      "cx-paracentral-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_precuneus": {
    "functionText": "The precuneus supports several forms of integration on the medial parietal surface, including visuospatial representations, imagery, and aspects of remembering personal events. Its anterior, central, and posterior territories participate in different networks. Some parts are prominent in internally directed cognition, whereas others relate more to sensorimotor or visual processing; the whole region is not uniformly default-mode cortex.",
    "connectionsText": "Precuneus subdivisions interact with posterior cingulate and medial prefrontal cortex, lateral parietal regions, visual association areas, and sensorimotor systems. Connections with the hippocampal memory network are often mediated through wider posteromedial and parahippocampal interactions. This differentiated connectivity helps relate body position, viewed space, and remembered context without requiring every precuneus parcel to share the same partners.",
    "clinicalText": "Posteromedial injury may affect spatial orientation, imagery, or memory-related processing, depending on the affected subdivision and connected pathways. Precuneus network changes are also studied in Alzheimer disease, but are not specific enough to diagnose it alone. Reports of a role in awareness describe broader network participation and should not be interpreted as locating consciousness in this gyrus.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-precuneus-dmn",
      "cx-posteromedial-ad",
      "cx-parietal-connectome",
      "cx-mtl-networks"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_rostral_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The rostral gyrus is a small medial frontal landmark within the neighborhood of ventromedial and subcallosal cortex. That broader system relates value, affect, and internal context to behavior. The narrow gyral label does not correspond to an independently validated mental operation, so its educational role is to locate this medial frontal interface without inventing a unique specialization.",
    "connectionsText": "Adjacent medial orbital, rostral cingulate, and frontopolar regions provide the immediate network context. Broader ventromedial frontal systems interact with amygdala, striatum, and temporal memory-related cortex. Evidence for these relationships comes mainly from larger cortical territories; direct human tracing of connections restricted to the rostral gyrus is insufficient to specify a separate, complete circuit.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions near this medial basal frontal region may accompany changes in motivation, affect, or value-guided behavior, especially when they involve neighboring cingulate or basal forebrain structures. No recognized syndrome is specific to the rostral gyrus alone. Clinical interpretation should emphasize the actual lesion boundaries and network damage rather than treating a small atlas highlight as a diagnosis.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-ofc-networks",
      "cx-acc-depression",
      "cx-ofc-lesions"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_short_insular_gyri": {
    "functionText": "The short insular gyri occupy much of the anterior insula, where bodily signals are integrated with emotion, attention, and the need to respond. Dorsal and ventral portions emphasize different cognitive and affective relationships. This territory is therefore more than a taste or emotion area, and the visible short gyri do not exactly reproduce the functional subdivision boundaries.",
    "connectionsText": "Anterior insular regions interact with anterior cingulate and frontal control systems, while ventral portions have stronger limbic and orbital relationships. Local insular pathways connect these regions with more posterior sensory representations. Structural studies identify overlapping frontotemporal association routes, supporting a coordinated network for linking bodily state and salient events with behavior rather than a single isolated insular pathway.",
    "clinicalText": "Anterior insular stimulation may evoke visceral sensations, anxiety, or changes in salience and action. Lesions can affect aspects of bodily awareness or emotional processing, with additional speech effects when dominant opercular connections are involved. The symptoms of an insular seizure may mimic temporal or frontal onset, so clinical localization requires evidence beyond the experienced sensation alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-insula-stimulation",
      "cx-insula-tracts",
      "cx-insula-gradient",
      "cx-insula-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_subcallosal_gyrus_parolfactory_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "This small subcallosal or parolfactory landmark lies beneath the front of the corpus callosum, near medial frontal and septal-basal forebrain territories. Its context is a network linking affective state, motivation, and bodily regulation. The anatomical parolfactory gyrus should not automatically be equated with the entire subcallosal cingulate region or with Brodmann area 25.",
    "connectionsText": "Neighboring medial orbital and anterior cingulate cortex link this area to a broader limbic frontal system. Studies of larger subcallosal cingulate territories identify relationships with frontal pole, cingulate cortex, thalamus, and nucleus accumbens. Those findings provide regional context, but neither a nearby tract nor a stimulation target proves that every connection terminates in this small parolfactory parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Subcallosal cingulate networks are studied in depression and experimental neuromodulation, but their findings cannot be transferred directly to this smaller anatomical gyrus. A lesion here may also involve basal forebrain or nearby vascular structures with different consequences. There is no established isolated parolfactory-gyrus syndrome, and the atlas label alone cannot identify a treatment target or explain mood symptoms.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-subcallosal-tracts",
      "cx-subcallosal-treatment",
      "cx-acc-depression"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_superior_frontal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The superior frontal gyrus includes regions involved in working memory, goal-directed control, and organization of movement. Anteromedial, dorsolateral, and posterior parts contribute differently, with posterior territory closer to motor systems. Its long anatomical extent therefore spans several computations, and a general executive label should not obscure distinctions between maintaining information, monitoring goals, and preparing actions.",
    "connectionsText": "Connectivity studies distinguish anteromedial links with anterior and middle cingulate cortex, dorsolateral links with middle and inferior frontal cortex, and posterior links with precentral cortex, caudate, thalamus, and frontal operculum. These patterns place different superior frontal sectors in control, default mode, and sensorimotor networks rather than assigning the entire gyrus one set of connections.",
    "clinicalText": "Focal left superior frontal lesions can impair aspects of working memory, while more posterior or medial lesions may affect initiation and movement organization. The clinical pattern depends on location and extent, including neighboring supplementary motor or cingulate tissue. Everyday planning difficulties can be present even when elementary motor testing or conversational language appears relatively preserved.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-sfg-connectivity",
      "cx-sfg-lesion",
      "cx-frontoparietal-control",
      "cx-aca-stroke"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_superior_occipital_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The superior occipital gyrus participates in dorsal extrastriate visual processing, contributing visual information used for spatial attention and visually guided behavior. Its territory lies near multiple visual field maps rather than constituting one motion or location center. Functional boundaries depend on individual anatomy and mapping, so the surface gyrus is best used as a regional orientation landmark.",
    "connectionsText": "Local connections link early occipital and other extrastriate visual areas, while dorsal pathways continue toward superior parietal and intraparietal regions. These interactions support transforming visual information into spatially appropriate actions and attentional priorities. Temporal visual associations are also part of the broader occipital network; the division into dorsal and ventral streams is useful but not an absolute anatomical separation.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury near the occipitoparietal junction can disrupt visually guided reaching or spatial processing, sometimes alongside visual-field deficits. Such effects often involve adjoining parietal cortex and cannot be assigned specifically to every superior occipital lesion. Testing should distinguish whether errors arise from impaired seeing, attention, coordinate transformation, or movement execution before attributing a particular higher-order visual syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-visual-maps",
      "cx-retinotopy",
      "cx-parietal-connectome",
      "cx-optic-ataxia"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_superior_temporal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The superior temporal gyrus supports auditory analysis and the interpretation of meaningful sounds, including speech. Its dorsal surface contains primary and association auditory territories, while lateral and posterior regions contribute to more complex acoustic and social information. Language-dominant and nondominant hemispheres often differ in emphasis, but speech, music, and prosody rely on interacting bilateral networks.",
    "connectionsText": "Auditory pathways connect Heschl's gyrus with anterior planum-polare and posterior planum-temporale regions and onward along superior temporal cortex. Posterior temporal interactions with inferior parietal and frontal regions support auditory-motor and language functions, while anterior temporal links contribute to recognition and meaning. Connections with neighboring middle temporal cortex further integrate sound with broader conceptual information.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant posterior temporal lesions can impair spoken-language comprehension, whereas other temporal lesions may affect nonverbal sounds, prosody, or auditory recognition. Severe cortical hearing loss usually reflects bilateral central auditory damage. The superior temporal gyrus is much larger than classical Wernicke territory, so a highlighted segment does not by itself establish the type or severity of an auditory-language disorder.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-auditory-streams",
      "cx-auditory-depth",
      "cx-planum-temporale",
      "cx-semantic-network",
      "cx-auditory-lesions",
      "cx-cortical-deafness"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_supramarginal_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "The supramarginal gyrus integrates sensory and action-related information within inferior parietal cortex. Left-sided regions contribute to phonological processing and learned skilled actions, while right-sided regions participate in spatial and body-related attention. These roles are distributed across subdivisions, making the whole gyrus broader than either a speech-sound store or a single body-schema center.",
    "connectionsText": "Short fibers link nearby postcentral, parietal opercular, angular, and posterior temporal regions. Superior longitudinal and arcuate systems provide longer connections with frontal and temporal cortex, with different components supporting different sensorimotor and language interactions. Human dissection and tractography show this region as a junction of several pathways rather than a single relay between two classical language centers.",
    "clinicalText": "Dominant inferior parietal injury can impair speech-sound handling, repetition, or performance of learned gestures, particularly when connecting fibers are damaged. Right-sided injury may contribute to neglect or altered body awareness. These syndromes often involve a wider network, so their presence does not identify one supramarginal subdivision, and their absence does not establish that the entire gyrus is intact.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-parietal-tracts",
      "cx-parietal-connectome",
      "cx-semantic-network",
      "cx-angular-functions"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_supraparietal_lobule": {
    "functionText": "This atlas label corresponds to superior parietal territory that combines visual and somatic information for spatial attention and control of reaching or grasping. It helps update where the hand and target are relative to one another as an action unfolds. Different subregions emphasize body representation, attention, or visuomotor transformation, rather than one undifferentiated spatial function.",
    "connectionsText": "Superior parietal regions interact with postcentral somatosensory cortex, dorsal occipital visual regions, intraparietal cortex, and dorsal premotor or other frontal action systems. Superior longitudinal pathways contribute to frontoparietal communication. These relationships allow current limb position and visual target information to shape movement, with additional thalamic and medial parietal interactions supporting the broader spatial-control network.",
    "clinicalText": "Posterior superior parietal injury can cause optic ataxia: inaccurate visually guided reaching that is not explained simply by weakness. Deficits may depend on the hand used and the visual field containing the target. Bilateral larger parietal lesions can produce a more extensive syndrome, while dominant-side injury may also affect skilled spatial aspects of writing or action.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-optic-ataxia",
      "cx-parietal-connectome",
      "cx-somatosensory-attention",
      "cx-gyral-anatomy"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_temporal_agranular_insular_cortex_area_Tl": {
    "functionText": "This temporal agranular insular parcel lies within the transition between insular and anterior temporal-limbic cortex. Its surrounding networks relate sensory and bodily information to emotional and mnemonic significance. Agranular is a description of cortical layering, not evidence of a unique mental faculty; functional findings from larger ventral insular regions should be applied here with appropriate anatomical caution.",
    "connectionsText": "The regional context includes anterior insula, temporal pole, amygdalar territory, and orbitofrontal systems, with local cortical links and nearby frontotemporal association fibers. The uncinate fasciculus passes through the adjacent basal insular junction. A passing tract is not equivalent to a projection ending in this parcel, and direct human connectivity evidence for its exact microscopic boundaries remains limited.",
    "clinicalText": "Disease or seizure activity in the surrounding insular-temporal network can involve visceral sensations, affective experiences, or memory-related symptoms. These manifestations may reflect neighboring structures or rapid propagation rather than this parcel alone. There is no established temporal-agranular-insula syndrome, and precise interpretation requires separating cortical involvement from injury to nearby amygdala, temporal stem, and opercular regions.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-insula-anatomy",
      "cx-insula-tracts",
      "cx-insula-stimulation",
      "cx-temporal-stem",
      "cx-allen-atlas"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_temporal_pole": {
    "functionText": "The temporal pole helps integrate information across modalities into knowledge about concepts, people, and socially meaningful situations. Left-sided anterior temporal systems often have a stronger relationship to words and naming, while right-sided systems contribute prominently to nonverbal person and social knowledge. These are relative tendencies within bilateral, internally differentiated networks rather than an absolute verbal-nonverbal split.",
    "connectionsText": "Local connections join superior, middle, and inferior temporal association regions. The inferior longitudinal fasciculus brings posterior visual associations forward, and the uncinate fasciculus links anterior temporal regions with orbital and rostral frontal cortex. Relationships with amygdala and fusiform regions connect conceptual knowledge with affect and perception, supporting interpretation of a face or voice in its broader context.",
    "clinicalText": "Anterior temporal degeneration, often left-predominant in semantic variant primary progressive aphasia, can progressively erode word meaning and conceptual knowledge. Right-predominant disease may prominently affect recognition of familiar people or social understanding. The syndrome reflects distributed and often bilateral network degeneration, so isolated temporal-pole involvement does not predict the full pattern or establish a diagnosis by itself.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-temporal-pole-degeneration",
      "cx-temporal-pole-social",
      "cx-uncinate-anatomy",
      "cx-ilf-anatomy",
      "cx-right-temporal-degeneration"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_transverse_temporal_gyrus_Heschls_gyrus": {
    "functionText": "Heschl's gyrus contains much of primary auditory cortex, where sound is represented through organized sensitivity to frequency and other acoustic features. It begins cortical analysis needed for speech, music, and environmental hearing, without being exclusively responsible for any one category. Duplication and other normal anatomical variation mean that the visible gyrus and microscopic primary auditory borders do not match perfectly.",
    "connectionsText": "Auditory thalamocortical projections from the medial geniculate body reach auditory cortex through the auditory radiations. Local connections distribute information toward posterior planum temporale and anterior planum polare, then into wider superior temporal networks. Both ascending input and cortical feedback contribute to processing, and the two hemispheres cooperate rather than assigning all speech to one side and all music to the other.",
    "clinicalText": "Unilateral injury may impair detailed auditory discrimination without eliminating hearing because central auditory pathways have substantial bilateral representation. Severe cortical deafness usually involves bilateral auditory cortical or related pathway damage. Auditory hallucinations or seizures may include simple sounds, but the experienced sound alone does not uniquely locate onset to Heschl's gyrus or distinguish cortical from other auditory causes.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "cx-auditory-streams",
      "cx-auditory-depth",
      "cx-gyral-anatomy",
      "cx-auditory-tonotopy",
      "cx-cortical-deafness"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_nuclear_complex_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The anterior thalamic complex helps organize recollection and spatial orientation within an extended memory network. Its anterodorsal, anteroventral, and anteromedial components make different contributions to direction, context, and memory-guided behavior. It is an active part of learning a route or remembering an event, rather than a storage compartment for all memories.",
    "connectionsText": "Mammillary bodies send a major input through the mammillothalamic tract; the hippocampal formation also provides direct input through the fornix. Reciprocal connections with retrosplenial and cingulate cortex link this diencephalic circuit to cortical representations of places and events. Different anterior nuclei participate in overlapping head-direction and hippocampal memory networks.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury involving the anterior thalamus or its mammillary connections can impair new learning and recollection, sometimes with disorientation. Left-sided damage may disproportionately affect verbal memory, although lesion extent and network disruption matter. Thalamic amnesia should not automatically be attributed to the mediodorsal nucleus when the anterior nuclei or mammillothalamic tract are also injured.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-anterior-memory",
      "deep-mtt-memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_basal_forebrain": {
    "functionText": "The basal forebrain is a collection of cell groups that regulates cortical readiness for attention, learning, and memory. Cholinergic neurons adjust how strongly relevant signals influence their targets, while intermingled GABAergic and glutamatergic populations contribute additional control. This broad atlas region therefore represents several interacting systems, not one uniform attention center.",
    "connectionsText": "The nucleus basalis and substantia innominata provide much of the acetylcholine reaching neocortex and amygdala. Medial septal and diagonal-band neurons innervate hippocampal and entorhinal circuits. Inputs from prefrontal and limbic cortex, hypothalamus, and brainstem neuromodulatory nuclei help these populations tune processing to current goals, wakefulness, and behavioral significance.",
    "clinicalText": "Basal forebrain cholinergic degeneration contributes to cognitive dysfunction in Alzheimer disease and other dementias, particularly impaired attention and memory encoding. It is one component of a much wider disease process. A visible basal forebrain parcel cannot identify cholinergic cell loss, and an isolated change in this region does not establish a dementia diagnosis.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-basal-forebrain"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_body_of_caudate": {
    "functionText": "The caudate body is the middle portion of the curved dorsal striatum beside the lateral ventricle. It participates in selecting learned actions and directing attention using sensory and associative information. Functional territories overlap along the caudate, so the anatomical division into head, body, and tail is not a set of three sharply separated processors.",
    "connectionsText": "Parietal association cortex and other cortical territories project to this intermediate caudate region, with topography that overlaps neighboring head and tail circuits. Dopamine from substantia nigra modulates striatal learning. GABAergic projection neurons communicate with pallidal and nigral targets, which influence cortical and orienting systems through thalamic and brainstem output pathways.",
    "clinicalText": "Caudate injury can disrupt attention, initiation, flexible behavior, and movement depending on the affected circuit. Huntington disease often involves the caudate broadly, while a focal vascular lesion may also affect nearby white matter. There is no established clinical syndrome unique to the atlas-defined caudate body; location must be interpreted with the full lesion and examination.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-caudate-topography",
      "deep-caudate-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_centromedian_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The centromedian nucleus is a posterior intralaminar thalamic nucleus involved in responding to behaviorally important events and adjusting ongoing actions. Together with parafascicular nuclei, it helps basal ganglia circuits notice unexpected information. Its stronger association with sensorimotor striatum distinguishes it from a primary sensory relay that simply transmits touch or vision.",
    "connectionsText": "Centromedian neurons provide excitatory input especially to the putamen, contacting striatal projection neurons and cholinergic interneurons. Brainstem arousal systems, motor-related cortex, and basal ganglia contribute inputs. This arrangement links vigilance and surprising events to the striatal circuits that select, interrupt, or update a response already under way.",
    "clinicalText": "The centromedian region is studied as a stimulation target in selected epilepsy and movement-disorder settings because it can influence distributed networks. This does not make it a single seizure generator. Lesions involving intralaminar thalamus may disturb alertness or behavior, but a deficit specifically confined to centromedian injury is difficult to establish in humans.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-cm-pf-learning",
      "deep-intralaminar-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_claustrum": {
    "functionText": "The claustrum is a thin sheet of gray matter between the insula and putamen. It is implicated in coordinating activity across cortical areas, including selection of relevant information during demanding tasks. Its precise contribution remains under investigation; descriptions of it as a proven single center of consciousness or sensory binding go beyond current evidence.",
    "connectionsText": "Extensive, regionally organized connections link claustrum with prefrontal, cingulate, visual, auditory, and somatosensory cortex. Connections with midline thalamic nuclei, including reuniens, place it near memory and executive networks. Much detailed wiring evidence comes from animal tracing, and a continuous atlas surface should not be interpreted as uniform connectivity across the entire structure.",
    "clinicalText": "Reported claustral abnormalities occur in several neurological conditions, but neighboring insular cortex and external or extreme capsule pathways are often affected too. This makes a unique claustral lesion syndrome uncertain. Clinical interpretation should distinguish the thin gray-matter sheet from adjacent tissue instead of assigning changes in consciousness, seizures, or cognition to the claustrum alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-claustrum"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_external_segment_of_globus_pallidus": {
    "functionText": "The external globus pallidus, or GPe, regulates activity inside the basal ganglia through continuously active inhibitory neurons. It helps shape when competing actions are suppressed or released and how striatal signals evolve over time. Multiple GPe cell populations have different targets, making it more than a passive relay in the textbook indirect pathway.",
    "connectionsText": "Striatal neurons provide a major inhibitory input, while the subthalamic nucleus provides excitation. GPe sends inhibitory projections to the subthalamic nucleus, internal pallidum, substantia nigra, and back to striatum. Reciprocal GPe–subthalamic connections create a recurrent circuit; striatal-projecting populations can regulate the input stage of basal ganglia processing itself.",
    "clinicalText": "Abnormal GPe firing patterns and synchronization participate in Parkinson disease and other movement disorders. The clinical effect of damage depends on which populations and neighboring pathways are affected, rather than simply whether GPe activity is higher or lower. GPe should be distinguished from the internal pallidal segment, a major output nucleus and established surgical target.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-gpe"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_habenular_nuclei": {
    "functionText": "The habenula contains medial and lateral nuclei that link forebrain state to brainstem neuromodulation. Lateral habenular circuits participate in learning from unfavorable outcomes and regulating motivational responses. Medial habenular circuits have different cellular organization and roles, including aversion-related processes. A shared anatomical label should not erase the distinction between these two systems.",
    "connectionsText": "Forebrain inputs reach the habenula through the stria medullaris, and outputs travel through the fasciculus retroflexus. The medial division projects prominently to the interpeduncular nucleus. The lateral division influences dopamine and serotonin systems through midbrain targets, including the rostromedial tegmental nucleus, linking limbic and basal ganglia information to reinforcement signals.",
    "clinicalText": "Altered lateral habenular signaling is investigated in depression and addiction, but these disorders are not localized to the habenula alone. Much mechanistic evidence comes from animal experiments, and isolated human lesions are uncommon. Neither habenular size nor activity on its own provides a diagnostic marker for mood, motivation, or a substance-use disorder.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-habenula"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_head_of_caudate": {
    "functionText": "The caudate head is the enlarged anterior portion of dorsal striatum. It helps translate goals, rules, and recently learned outcomes into action choices. Compared with the tail, it is more closely associated with flexible selection when circumstances change. This distinction is a functional gradient, with substantial overlap between cognitive, motivational, and movement-related territories.",
    "connectionsText": "Prefrontal and cingulate cortex provide major associative inputs, alongside thalamic signals and dopamine from substantia nigra. Caudate output reaches internal pallidum and substantia nigra through direct pathways, or external pallidum through indirect pathways. Thalamic and superior-colliculus connections then influence cortical planning and the selection of orienting eye movements.",
    "clinicalText": "Caudate-head lesions can produce reduced initiative, slowed thinking, perseveration, or difficulty changing strategy; movement abnormalities may also occur. Findings vary with laterality and involvement of internal-capsule or frontal connections. Primate experiments on flexible object value help explain the circuit, but do not establish a one-to-one human symptom for every caudate-head lesion.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-caudate-flexible",
      "deep-caudate-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_hypothalamus": {
    "functionText": "The hypothalamus coordinates internal bodily conditions with behavior. Its distinct nuclei regulate fluid balance, temperature, feeding, circadian timing, sleep, stress responses, and reproduction. It also controls pituitary hormone release. These functions belong to interacting cell groups and pathways; the whole hypothalamus is not interchangeable with any one nucleus or with the overlying thalamus.",
    "connectionsText": "Blood-borne signals and inputs from limbic cortex, amygdala, hippocampus, retina, and visceral brainstem systems inform hypothalamic circuits. Outputs reach the pituitary through neurosecretory axons or the median-eminence portal circulation. Descending projections to brainstem and spinal autonomic centers coordinate endocrine changes with heart rate, temperature regulation, feeding, and other bodily responses.",
    "clinicalText": "Hypothalamic injury can combine endocrine abnormalities with altered thirst, appetite, temperature, sleep, or autonomic regulation. Craniopharyngioma and nearby surgery are examples in which several of these systems may be affected together. The pattern depends on the nuclei and pituitary connections involved; a broad hypothalamic location does not predict one fixed clinical syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-hypothalamus-anatomy",
      "deep-hypothalamus-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_internal_segment_of_globus_pallidus": {
    "functionText": "The internal globus pallidus, or GPi, is a major output station of the basal ganglia. Its inhibitory neurons continuously influence downstream targets, and patterned changes in that inhibition help regulate movement selection and scaling. Motor, associative, and limbic territories coexist, so GPi output contributes to more than muscle control alone.",
    "connectionsText": "GPi receives inhibitory striatal and external-pallidal inputs and excitatory input from the subthalamic nucleus. Its GABAergic output reaches motor thalamus through pallidothalamic pathways, including the ansa lenticularis and lenticular fasciculus. Thalamic projections return influence to frontal motor areas; additional pallidal outputs reach brainstem systems involved in posture and locomotion.",
    "clinicalText": "Abnormal pallidal output contributes to hypokinetic and hyperkinetic movement disorders. GPi is a stimulation or lesion target in selected patients with Parkinson disease or dystonia, illustrating its influence over a wider motor network. Clinical effects depend on territory and nearby fibers, and the mechanisms of stimulation cannot be reduced to simply switching GPi off.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-bg-circuits",
      "deep-basal-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_dorsal_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The lateral dorsal thalamic nucleus participates in spatial learning and orientation, linking visual context to memory-related cortical processing. It shares several circuit features with the anterior thalamic nuclei but is a separate structure. Its role is better understood as part of a distributed navigation and memory system than as a general sensory relay.",
    "connectionsText": "Reciprocal connections link the lateral dorsal nucleus with retrosplenial and cingulate cortex and the hippocampal formation. Visual and other association inputs can inform these memory-related circuits about the surrounding environment. Its connectivity therefore places it between representations of current spatial cues and networks that use those cues to orient and remember.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage near the lateral dorsal nucleus may contribute to impaired spatial memory or orientation, but isolated human lesions are uncommon. Adjacent anterior nuclei, pulvinar, or connecting fibers often complicate localization. Animal evidence supports a memory role; it does not justify assigning every navigation problem or amnestic syndrome to this small nucleus specifically.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-thalamus-cognition"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_posterior_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The lateral posterior nucleus belongs to the posterior association thalamus. It helps link spatial and sensory representations with attention and visually guided action, in close relationship with parietal cortex and the pulvinar. Its boundaries and subdivisions vary across species and atlases, so findings from a rodent lateral-posterior complex do not map directly onto this human parcel.",
    "connectionsText": "Primate tracing shows strong lateral-posterior inputs to posterior parietal zones involved in reaching, grasping, and defensive movements. These connections overlap with anterior pulvinar projections and help bring thalamic information into cortical action maps. Corticothalamic feedback makes this a recurrent association circuit rather than a one-direction pathway carrying a single sensory modality.",
    "clinicalText": "A posterior thalamic lesion can affect spatial attention or sensorimotor integration, but an isolated lateral-posterior syndrome is not well established. Neighboring pulvinar and sensory nuclei frequently share the lesion. Interpretation should use the complete vascular or structural lesion and behavioral findings instead of inferring a specific impairment from the atlas label alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-lp-parietal"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_mammillary_region_of_HTH": {
    "functionText": "This posterior hypothalamic region contains the mammillary bodies and neighboring hypothalamic tissue. Mammillary nuclei contribute to recollection and spatial orientation, with medial and lateral divisions carrying different signals. The larger regional parcel also lies near arousal and autonomic systems, so its function is broader than the paired rounded mammillary bodies visible on the brain base.",
    "connectionsText": "Mammillary nuclei receive hippocampal-subicular information through the fornix and inputs from tegmental nuclei of the brainstem. The mammillothalamic tract carries output to anterior thalamic nuclei, while the mammillotegmental tract links back to tegmental systems. These pathways couple memory-related cortical networks with signals relevant to orientation, movement, and neural timing.",
    "clinicalText": "Mammillary injury is a characteristic component of Wernicke–Korsakoff pathology and can accompany substantial memory impairment, but the disorder usually affects a broader network. Posterior hypothalamic damage beyond the bodies may additionally alter arousal or autonomic regulation. The clinical meaning of this parcel depends on whether the mammillary nuclei, their tracts, or neighboring tissue is affected.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-mammillary",
      "deep-hypothalamus-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_mammillothalamic_tract": {
    "functionText": "The mammillothalamic tract is a compact white-matter pathway carrying mammillary output to the anterior thalamus. It supports the coordination of memory and orientation networks rather than performing computation as a gray-matter nucleus. Its importance illustrates how damage to a small connecting tract can disrupt a much larger system for learning and recollection.",
    "connectionsText": "Fibers ascend from medial and lateral mammillary nuclei to their anterior thalamic targets. Those targets communicate with retrosplenial and cingulate cortex and the hippocampal formation, embedding the tract in the extended hippocampal memory circuit. It should be distinguished from the fornix entering the mammillary region and from mammillotegmental fibers directed toward the brainstem.",
    "clinicalText": "Mammillothalamic damage can contribute to diencephalic amnesia even when much thalamic gray matter is spared. Experimental lesions impair spatial-memory tasks, while human lesions are often vascular and may involve neighboring anterior thalamic tissue. Small tract injury can be missed on coarse images, so the visible size of a lesion need not match its cognitive impact.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-mtt-memory",
      "deep-anterior-memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_mediodorsal_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The mediodorsal nucleus supports prefrontal operations such as working memory, flexible rule use, and learning decisions from outcomes. It helps sustain and update activity across frontal networks rather than merely passing information to them. Its subdivisions connect to different prefrontal territories, giving the nucleus distinct cognitive, motivational, and memory-related roles across its extent.",
    "connectionsText": "Reciprocal projections join mediodorsal thalamus with dorsolateral, orbitofrontal, medial prefrontal, and cingulate cortex. Inputs also come from limbic and basal ganglia systems, with patterns differing by subdivision. These pathways permit information about goals, recent experience, and value to influence prefrontal processing and allow cortical feedback to regulate thalamic contributions.",
    "clinicalText": "Focal mediodorsal injury may impair executive control, strategic retrieval, and learning, resembling aspects of frontal dysfunction. Severe thalamic amnesia often includes additional damage to anterior nuclei or mammillothalamic connections. This distinction matters: memory difficulties after a medial thalamic stroke do not prove that mediodorsal thalamus is a standalone memory-storage center.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-mediodorsal"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_nucleus_accumbens": {
    "functionText": "The nucleus accumbens is a ventral striatal region that helps convert motivational significance into approach, effort, and learned behavior. Its core and shell differ in circuitry and contributions. It participates in learning about both rewarding and aversive events; describing it simply as a pleasure center misses its roles in anticipation, context, and action selection.",
    "connectionsText": "Prefrontal cortex, basolateral amygdala, hippocampal-subicular regions, and thalamus provide excitatory inputs, while the ventral tegmental area supplies dopamine. Outputs reach ventral pallidum and midbrain; shell territories also connect with hypothalamic and extended-amygdala systems. These converging pathways bring goals, emotional significance, and environmental context into motivationally driven behavior.",
    "clinicalText": "Drug-associated adaptations in accumbens circuits can strengthen cue-triggered seeking and contribute to addiction. Altered motivation also involves this region in other conditions, but neither a symptom nor a scan can be assigned to accumbens alone. Addiction is a distributed learning and control disorder, and mechanistic findings from specific animal core or shell circuits need careful translation.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-accumbens"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_parafascicular_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The parafascicular nucleus is part of the posterior intralaminar thalamus. It helps striatal circuits respond when an expected action–outcome relationship changes, supporting attention and behavioral updating. Compared with centromedian thalamus, its primate projections emphasize more associative striatal territories. The two nuclei are related but should not be treated as identical motor relays.",
    "connectionsText": "Parafascicular neurons send excitatory thalamostriatal projections, including to caudate and striatal cholinergic interneurons. Cortical, basal ganglia, and brainstem inputs convey information about ongoing behavior and salient events. By influencing interneurons as well as projection neurons, this system can alter how striatum processes cortical input when a learned response needs revision.",
    "clinicalText": "Parafascicular and centromedian degeneration is documented in Parkinson disease, adding to the effects of dopamine loss on striatal function. However, most human lesion and stimulation studies involve both nuclei or neighboring tissue. A parafascicular-specific clinical syndrome remains uncertain, and broader cognitive or movement symptoms should not be explained by this nucleus in isolation.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-thalamostriatal"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_pineal_body": {
    "functionText": "The pineal body is an endocrine gland that releases melatonin in a daily pattern, usually higher during biological night. This hormone communicates timing information to the body and helps coordinate sleep-related rhythms. The gland is influenced by the circadian clock rather than serving as the clock itself, and it does not directly receive conscious visual images.",
    "connectionsText": "Light information reaches the hypothalamic suprachiasmatic clock through retinal input. A multisynaptic autonomic pathway then involves hypothalamic output, upper thoracic spinal sympathetic neurons, and the superior cervical ganglion before reaching the pineal gland. Melatonin enters the circulation, linking environmental light timing with endocrine signals that act on many tissues.",
    "clinicalText": "A pineal-region mass can compress the dorsal midbrain, producing impaired upward gaze and other features of Parinaud syndrome. Compression of the cerebral aqueduct can cause obstructive hydrocephalus. These signs reflect neighboring structures, not loss of melatonin alone; many small pineal cysts are incidental and do not produce this clinical pattern.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-pineal",
      "deep-parinaud"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_posteroventral_putamen": {
    "functionText": "This label marks a posterior and ventral anatomical portion of the putamen. Posterior putaminal territories participate strongly in sensorimotor processing and the performance of practiced actions. The parcel is not a separate nucleus, and its boundary should not be read as the exact edge of a body-part representation or a uniquely defined movement function.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior putamen receives converging inputs from motor, premotor, supplementary motor, and somatosensory cortical systems, with overlapping somatotopic organization. Dopamine from substantia nigra modifies learning and response vigor. Striatal outputs reach pallidal and nigral nuclei, which return influence to frontal motor cortex through motor thalamus and also engage brainstem motor networks.",
    "clinicalText": "Posterior putaminal dopamine dysfunction is especially relevant to motor manifestations of Parkinson disease, although the strongest early depletion is often described dorsoposteriorly rather than throughout this ventral parcel. A putaminal stroke may also affect adjacent internal-capsule fibers. Neither parkinsonism nor weakness should therefore be attributed exclusively to this atlas-defined subdivision.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-putamen",
      "deep-parkinson-imaging"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_preoptic_region_of_HTH": {
    "functionText": "The preoptic region coordinates body-temperature regulation and sleep with other homeostatic and reproductive functions. Different neuronal populations detect thermal signals or promote sleep, and these populations only partly overlap. The region is therefore a collection of specialized circuits near the front of the hypothalamus, rather than a single switch for sleep or temperature.",
    "connectionsText": "Thermal information reaches preoptic circuits through spinal and lateral parabrachial pathways and through sensitivity to local brain temperature. Outputs to dorsomedial hypothalamus and medullary raphe systems regulate heat production and loss. Sleep-related populations influence hypothalamic and brainstem arousal nuclei, while distinct reproductive pathways communicate with neuroendocrine systems.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury affecting preoptic or neighboring anterior hypothalamic tissue can disturb temperature regulation and sleep. Most detailed cellular evidence comes from experimental animals, and human lesions rarely isolate one preoptic population. Insomnia or abnormal temperature alone cannot localize damage here; inflammation, endocrine factors, and wider hypothalamic or brainstem involvement can produce similar findings.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-preoptic",
      "deep-preoptic-connections"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_pulvinar_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The pulvinar is a large posterior thalamic association complex that helps coordinate attention and communication between cortical areas. Visual subdivisions contribute to selecting relevant locations or objects and filtering distraction. Medial and other subdivisions engage broader association networks, so the whole pulvinar should not be equated with the primary visual relay of the lateral geniculate nucleus.",
    "connectionsText": "Reciprocal projections link pulvinar subdivisions with visual cortex, temporal association cortex, posterior parietal cortex, and frontal eye fields. Superior-colliculus input reaches particular visual territories. This wiring allows cortical goals and incoming visual events to influence one another, supporting the coordination of orienting, object processing, and attention across multiple cortical regions.",
    "clinicalText": "Pulvinar injury can impair directing attention toward the opposite side of space or increase vulnerability to distractors. Severe neglect usually reflects a wider attention network, and deficits may change over time. Subdivision and lesion extent matter: a pulvinar lesion is not equivalent to destruction of the lateral geniculate relay or primary visual cortex.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-pulvinar"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_putamen": {
    "functionText": "The putamen is a major input structure of the basal ganglia that helps learn, select, and scale actions. Posterior territories are strongly sensorimotor, whereas more anterior territories participate in associative processing. Its neurons integrate cortical information and reinforcement signals, supporting practiced movement sequences as well as adjustments when an action's consequences change.",
    "connectionsText": "Motor, somatosensory, premotor, and association cortices project to the putamen, along with excitatory thalamic inputs. Dopaminergic nigrostriatal fibers modify striatal signaling and plasticity. Inhibitory output reaches internal pallidum and substantia nigra directly or external pallidum through indirect circuits, ultimately shaping thalamic feedback to cortex rather than directly commanding muscles.",
    "clinicalText": "Putaminal dysfunction contributes to Parkinson disease, Huntington disease, and other movement disorders, with different mechanisms and distributions. Hemorrhage in this region may extend into the internal capsule, explaining major contralateral weakness that is not a pure putaminal function. The relationship between structural damage and movement symptoms depends on the surrounding circuit and time course.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-putamen",
      "deep-bg-circuits",
      "deep-basal-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_reuniens_nucleus_medioventral_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "Nucleus reuniens lies in the ventral midline thalamus and supports coordination between memory and executive systems. Experimental work links it to spatial working memory, temporal organization, and using context to guide behavior. The atlas's combined terminology identifies a small midline parcel; it should not be confused with the much larger mediodorsal nucleus.",
    "connectionsText": "Reuniens receives medial prefrontal and subicular inputs and sends excitatory projections to hippocampal CA1, subiculum, and medial prefrontal cortex. Some neurons can influence more than one target. This arrangement supplies an indirect route through which prefrontal goals can regulate hippocampal processing, within reciprocal circuits rather than a simple memory relay.",
    "clinicalText": "Reuniens circuits are investigated in epilepsy, memory disorders, and psychiatric disease, but most causal evidence comes from rodents. Selective human lesions are rare and difficult to resolve on routine imaging. A distinct human reuniens syndrome is therefore not established; clinical interpretation should emphasize the wider hippocampal–prefrontal network and the limits of anatomical localization.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-reuniens"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_septal_nuclei": {
    "functionText": "Septal nuclei include medial and lateral groups with different roles in limbic processing. The medial septal system helps organize hippocampal rhythmic activity and memory-related processing. Lateral septal circuits integrate contextual information with motivational and bodily responses. These gray-matter nuclei should not be confused with the septum pellucidum, the thin membrane between the lateral ventricles.",
    "connectionsText": "Medial septal and diagonal-band neurons send cholinergic, GABAergic, and glutamatergic projections to hippocampal circuits through fimbria–fornix pathways. The lateral septum receives strong hippocampal input and communicates with hypothalamic, preoptic, and tegmental regions. These pathways link representations of context and location to arousal, movement, feeding, and defensive or social behavior.",
    "clinicalText": "Septal or septohippocampal disruption can affect memory-related network activity, but focal human septal lesions are uncommon and frequently involve nearby fornix or basal forebrain tissue. Many proposed links to anxiety or social behavior derive from animal circuit experiments. They should not be interpreted as a localized human personality trait or a diagnostic signature.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-septal",
      "deep-lateral-septum"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_substantia_nigra": {
    "functionText": "The substantia nigra contains two major components with different jobs. Pars compacta supplies dopamine that modulates striatal learning and movement-related processing. Pars reticulata is a largely inhibitory basal ganglia output nucleus involved in action and eye-movement selection. A single surface label combines these populations and does not show their cellular boundaries or transmitter identity.",
    "connectionsText": "Pars compacta sends nigrostriatal dopamine projections to caudate and putamen. Pars reticulata receives striatal and pallidal inhibition plus subthalamic excitation, then projects to thalamus, superior colliculus, and brainstem targets. This separates a modulatory learning pathway from an output pathway that controls access to cortical and orienting circuits.",
    "clinicalText": "Degeneration of pars compacta dopamine neurons is central to the motor features of Parkinson disease, including slowness and rigidity, while the disease extends beyond this structure. Nigral imaging changes cannot alone establish the diagnosis. Midbrain lesions affecting nearby eye-movement or descending motor pathways can add signs that do not arise from nigral dysfunction itself.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-nigra-clinical",
      "deep-bg-circuits"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_subthalamic_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The subthalamic nucleus is an excitatory component of the basal ganglia that helps regulate action suppression and selection. Its motor, associative, and limbic territories contribute to different behaviors. It participates in rapid cortical control through the hyperdirect pathway as well as recurrent indirect-pathway processing, rather than simply turning all movement off.",
    "connectionsText": "Cortex supplies direct excitatory input through the hyperdirect pathway, while external globus pallidus provides inhibition. Subthalamic neurons send glutamatergic projections to internal pallidum and substantia nigra pars reticulata and communicate reciprocally with external pallidum. These pathways change basal ganglia output to thalamic and brainstem systems during action control.",
    "clinicalText": "A unilateral subthalamic lesion can produce contralateral hemiballismus, although similar movements can arise from lesions elsewhere in the connected network. The nucleus is also a deep-brain-stimulation target in selected Parkinson disease patients. Effects depend on territory and adjacent fibers; motor benefit and cognitive or mood changes cannot be predicted from stimulation location alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-stn",
      "deep-stn-control"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_supraoptic_region_of_HTH": {
    "functionText": "The supraoptic region is an anterior hypothalamic territory near the optic chiasm, not just the supraoptic nucleus. It includes neurosecretory and circadian cell groups with distinct roles. Supraoptic and paraventricular magnocellular neurons make vasopressin and oxytocin, while nearby suprachiasmatic circuitry coordinates daily timing; these functions should remain distinguishable within the regional label.",
    "connectionsText": "Magnocellular axons descend through the hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract to release hormones from the posterior pituitary. Osmotic and visceral inputs help regulate vasopressin secretion. Separately, retinal input reaches the suprachiasmatic nucleus through the retinohypothalamic tract, and hypothalamic outputs distribute timing signals to sleep, autonomic, and endocrine systems across the brain and body.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury to vasopressin-producing neurons or their pituitary axons can cause deficient water conservation with excessive dilute urine and thirst. Damage to neighboring circadian circuitry may alter sleep–wake timing. A lesion covering this regional parcel can combine problems, but neither symptom proves that the supraoptic nucleus alone is affected; pituitary-stalk and other causes must be distinguished.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-hypothalamus-anatomy",
      "deep-hypothalamus-human",
      "deep-hypothalamus-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_tail_of_caudate": {
    "functionText": "The caudate tail is the narrow posterior continuation of dorsal striatum curving into the temporal lobe. Primate experiments associate it with learned visual-object values that remain stable over time and guide automatic looking. This contrasts with more flexible value updating in the head, while remaining a research-supported circuit distinction rather than an absolute anatomical boundary.",
    "connectionsText": "Temporal and visual association cortices provide important inputs, with dopamine modulating learning within the circuit. Outputs reach caudal substantia nigra pars reticulata directly and through external-pallidal routes. Nigral projections influence the superior colliculus, allowing stored information about valuable or unhelpful visual objects to bias which objects receive orienting eye movements.",
    "clinicalText": "Selective human caudate-tail lesions are rare, and no simple clinical syndrome uniquely identifies this subdivision. Its contribution to learned orienting is established most directly in nonhuman-primate experiments. Broader caudate degeneration can affect cognition and action, but one should not infer a specific object-memory or eye-movement deficit solely from this atlas parcel.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-caudate-stable",
      "deep-caudate-object-pathways"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The thalamus is a collection of specialized nuclei that regulates communication among sensory, motor, cognitive, and arousal systems. Some nuclei relay defined sensory signals; others coordinate cortical networks or memory-related processing. Thalamic activity depends strongly on feedback and brain state, so describing the entire structure as a passive sensory relay is incomplete.",
    "connectionsText": "Most thalamic nuclei have reciprocal connections with particular cortical territories. Sensory nuclei receive ascending inputs, motor nuclei receive cerebellar or basal ganglia signals, and association nuclei connect with prefrontal, parietal, or limbic cortex. Brainstem arousal inputs and inhibition from the thalamic reticular nucleus help regulate the timing and selectivity of these interactions.",
    "clinicalText": "A thalamic stroke may cause contralateral sensory loss, altered alertness, memory difficulty, language changes, or neglect depending on its territory. Some sensory lesions lead to persistent central pain. Bilateral paramedian injury can markedly disturb consciousness, while a small lateral lesion may primarily affect sensation; the nucleus and pathway involved matter more than the word thalamus alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-thalamic-nuclei",
      "deep-thalamus-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_tuberal_region_of_HTH": {
    "functionText": "The tuberal region is the middle hypothalamic territory around the tuber cinereum and infundibulum. It contains arcuate, ventromedial, and dorsomedial cell groups with different contributions to energy balance, endocrine regulation, and autonomic responses. These are interacting circuits sensitive to nutritional and hormonal state, not a single hunger or satiety center.",
    "connectionsText": "Arcuate and neighboring neurons integrate circulating metabolic signals with inputs from other hypothalamic and brainstem regions. Projections to paraventricular and lateral hypothalamic circuits influence feeding and autonomic output. Neurosecretory pathways at the median eminence regulate anterior pituitary hormones, while connections with preoptic and brainstem systems coordinate energy expenditure and bodily responses.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage involving tuberal hypothalamic circuits can contribute to severe weight dysregulation and pituitary-hormone disturbances, especially after lesions or treatment near the hypothalamus. The outcome depends on which nuclei and connections are disrupted. A regional mass or injury may affect appetite, energy expenditure, and endocrine function together rather than producing one isolated feeding symptom.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-tuberal",
      "deep-hypothalamus-human"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_ventral_anterior_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The ventral anterior nucleus is part of motor-related thalamus and helps bring basal ganglia signals into frontal circuits for action preparation and selection. Its connections emphasize premotor and related frontal territories rather than a direct command to spinal muscles. Nuclear boundaries and names differ across atlases, particularly near the adjoining ventrolateral complex.",
    "connectionsText": "Inhibitory output from internal globus pallidus and substantia nigra reaches ventral-anterior motor-thalamic territories. Thalamocortical projections communicate with premotor, supplementary motor, and prefrontal systems, and cortical feedback returns to the thalamus. This recurrent basal ganglia–thalamocortical organization helps coordinate intended actions with contextual and learned information rather than merely relaying a finished motor plan.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury within anterior motor thalamus can disturb initiation or organization of movement, sometimes alongside cognitive changes when neighboring nuclei are involved. A pure ventral-anterior lesion syndrome is difficult to isolate in humans. Slowness, apathy, or weakness therefore requires assessment of the wider frontal–subcortical circuit and nearby pathways rather than attribution to this nucleus alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-va-connections",
      "deep-motor-thalamus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_ventral_lateral_nucleus_of_thalamus": {
    "functionText": "The ventral lateral nucleus contributes to the preparation and execution of coordinated movement. Different territories combine predominantly cerebellar or pallidal influences with cortical activity, supporting timing, scaling, and adaptation. The atlas displays a broad nucleus, while physiological and surgical subdivisions within motor thalamus are finer and are not identical across naming systems.",
    "connectionsText": "Deep cerebellar nuclei send output through the superior cerebellar peduncle to motor-thalamic territories, while internal pallidum supplies inhibitory input to more anterior regions. Ventral-lateral outputs reach primary motor and premotor cortex, with reciprocal cortical feedback. Human tractography shows overlapping distributions, so cerebellar and pallidal zones should not be portrayed as perfectly separated compartments.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions affecting cerebellothalamic territories can produce contralateral incoordination or tremor-related abnormalities. Surgical targeting of ventral-intermediate motor-thalamic territory can reduce selected tremors, but that target is not equivalent to the entire VL parcel. Clinical effects depend on involved tracts and adjacent sensory or motor pathways as well as thalamic gray matter.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-vl-connections",
      "deep-motor-thalamus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_zona_incerta": {
    "functionText": "The zona incerta is a heterogeneous sheet of gray matter below the thalamus. It participates in regulating sensory access, defensive and feeding behaviors, arousal, and movement-related activity. Different cell populations and territories have different roles, with much evidence coming from animal experiments; its name should not imply a single global behavioral control center.",
    "connectionsText": "Zona incerta receives cortical, subcortical, and brainstem inputs and sends prominent inhibitory projections to higher-order thalamic nuclei and other targets. Connections with superior colliculus, hypothalamic regions, and reticular systems link sensory information to behavioral state and motor responses. Its anatomical position also places it beside major cerebellothalamic and pallidothalamic fiber pathways.",
    "clinicalText": "The caudal zona-incerta region is investigated or targeted in surgery for selected tremor and movement disorders. Benefit may involve nearby passing fibers as well as incertal neurons, which limits simple localization claims. There is no single established syndrome for an isolated zona-incerta lesion, and proposed sensory or emotional roles should be interpreted with their evidence level in mind.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "deep-zona-incerta",
      "deep-zona-clinical"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_amygdalohippocampal_area": {
    "functionText": "This transition area sits where the posterior amygdala meets the anterior hippocampal formation. It is positioned to combine remembered context with emotional and motivational significance. Animal experiments implicate it in social and defensive behavior, but its boundaries and functions are less securely established in humans than those of the major amygdala nuclei.",
    "connectionsText": "Tracing studies, principally in rodents, describe links with ventral CA1 and subiculum, basal and medial amygdala, bed nucleus of the stria terminalis, and hypothalamic regions. These connections suggest an interface between contextual memory and bodily or behavioral responses. They should not be read as a fully demonstrated human wiring diagram for this small atlas parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "This region may be involved when temporal lobe epilepsy or surgery affects the amygdala–hippocampal border. Memory and emotional changes then reflect injury to a connected medial temporal network. There is no well-established isolated human lesion syndrome for this area, and a small segmented volume cannot establish a diagnosis or a specific behavioral trait.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdalohippocampal",
      "lb_amygdala_memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_amygdaloid_complex": {
    "functionText": "The amygdaloid complex comprises several nuclei that evaluate the biological importance of sensory events and support learning about threat, reward, and social signals. Different subdivisions contribute differently to memory, attention, and bodily responses. It is therefore more informative to think of interacting circuits than a single center that produces fear or any other emotion.",
    "connectionsText": "The lateral and basal nuclei exchange information with sensory association cortex, hippocampal regions, and prefrontal cortex. Central and medial components communicate with hypothalamus and brainstem. Major connecting routes include the stria terminalis, ventral amygdalofugal pathway, and uncinate fasciculus; these routes link evaluation of an event with memory, action selection, and autonomic adjustments.",
    "clinicalText": "Bilateral amygdala injury can impair aspects of recognizing emotional significance and learning from aversive events, although effects vary with task and surrounding damage. The complex also participates in temporal lobe seizure networks. Imaging associations with anxiety and other conditions are group findings, not a way to infer a person’s diagnosis or emotions from anatomy.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala",
      "lb_amygdala_dissection",
      "lb_amygdala_human"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_amygdaloid_area": {
    "functionText": "The anterior amygdaloid area is a small, heterogeneous region at the rostral edge of the amygdala, rather than one of its large, sharply differentiated basal nuclei. It contributes to the transition between olfactory, basal forebrain, and amygdala circuitry. Precise human functional assignments remain limited, so a unique psychological role would overstate the evidence.",
    "connectionsText": "Primate tracing identifies hypothalamic inputs, including projections associated with lateral and ventromedial hypothalamic territories. Comparative work also places the area among olfactory-related amygdala connections and local links with neighboring nuclei. These observations support integration of internal state and sensory significance, while species differences and changing nuclear definitions limit one-to-one translation to the human atlas label.",
    "clinicalText": "An isolated lesion syndrome has not been established for this small area. Anterior medial temporal surgery, seizures, or regional pathology may involve it alongside the uncus, piriform cortex, and other amygdala components. Any clinical interpretation should therefore describe the surrounding network and lesion extent instead of assigning a symptom exclusively to this parcel.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_hypothalamus",
      "lb_amygdala_feedback",
      "lb_amygdala_atlas"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_commissure": {
    "functionText": "The anterior commissure is a compact bundle crossing the midline in front of the fornix columns. It contributes to communication between temporal and olfactory-related regions of the two hemispheres. It is much smaller than the corpus callosum and has a different set of connections, so it should not be presented as a general substitute for all interhemispheric communication.",
    "connectionsText": "Its fibers connect portions of anterior and inferior temporal cortex and olfactory-related territories, with branches relating to the amygdala. Olfactory circuits use commissural routes to coordinate information between sides. The bundle’s close relationship to basal forebrain nuclei and fornix makes it an important anatomical landmark, even though those neighboring structures perform different functions.",
    "clinicalText": "Selective acquired injury is uncommon and does not have one consistently recognizable clinical syndrome. Its size and organization may differ when the corpus callosum is congenitally absent, but such variation does not establish how well an individual compensates. Lesions in this region often involve basal forebrain or fornix fibers, which can contribute more directly to memory deficits.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_commissures",
      "lb_amygdala_dissection",
      "lb_olfactory_wiring"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_cortical_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The anterior cortical nucleus is a superficial amygdala subdivision associated with olfactory processing. It helps place odor information within circuits concerned with biological significance and learned associations. It is distinct from the large basal nuclei that receive extensive multimodal association inputs; human evidence is strongest for its anatomical relationships, with finer behavioral specialization still under investigation.",
    "connectionsText": "Comparative tracing demonstrates olfactory bulb input to anterior cortical amygdala, and human diffusion imaging supports a relatively strong bulb–anterior cortical relationship. Local connections associate it with other olfactory amygdala regions and piriform-related networks. Diffusion streamlines show probable routes, however, and cannot prove synapses, transmission direction, or whether a pathway is monosynaptic in a particular person.",
    "clinicalText": "Smell-related emotional associations can be disturbed by broader olfactory or medial temporal disease, but a distinctive syndrome from an isolated anterior cortical nucleus lesion is not established. The small size of this nucleus also limits routine imaging precision. Clinical attribution requires information about neighboring amygdala, piriform cortex, olfactory pathways, and the patient’s actual sensory findings.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_olfactory",
      "lb_amygdala_olfactory_network"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_horn_of_lateral_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The anterior, or frontal, horn is the fluid-filled extension of the lateral ventricle into the frontal lobe. It contains cerebrospinal fluid and belongs to the ventricular system that supports the brain’s fluid environment. It is a cavity rather than a neural processor: frontal cognitive functions belong to surrounding tissue, not to the fluid space itself.",
    "connectionsText": "The horn continues posteriorly into the ventricular body near the interventricular foramen of Monro. Corpus callosum, septum pellucidum, and caudate head define important boundaries. These are spatial relationships, not synaptic connections. Fluid in the lateral ventricle ultimately communicates with the third ventricle through Monro, connecting this anterior recess to the wider CSF system.",
    "clinicalText": "Frontal horn enlargement is one measure used when assessing ventriculomegaly, but size alone does not distinguish hydrocephalus from tissue loss or normal variation. The horn is also a common target for ventricular catheter placement. Symptoms from expansion or nearby lesions arise from pressure and surrounding neural injury, requiring interpretation of the full scan and clinical findings.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_lateral_ventricles",
      "lb_ventricles",
      "lb_csf_disorders"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_anterior_olfactory_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "Despite its name, the anterior olfactory nucleus is a cortex-like olfactory region in and around the olfactory peduncle. It helps coordinate and modulate odor representations, including communication between the two sides. Animal studies reveal roles in odor memory and context-sensitive processing; human functional assignments are still less detailed than its established position in the olfactory network.",
    "connectionsText": "It receives olfactory bulb output and sends extensive feedback to bulb circuits. Connections with piriform and other olfactory regions integrate cortical processing, while fibers through the anterior commissure link the opposite olfactory system. This arrangement supports bilateral coordination and top-down modulation, rather than a strictly one-way relay from nose to cortex.",
    "clinicalText": "The anterior olfactory nucleus is investigated in neurodegenerative disorders associated with smell loss, including Parkinsonian and Alzheimer-type pathology. Such involvement is not a specific explanation for any one person’s impaired smell, which has many peripheral and central causes. A selective human lesion syndrome is poorly characterized, and its small subdivisions are difficult to assess with routine imaging.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_aon",
      "lb_olfactory_human"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_atrium_of_lateral_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The atrium, also called the trigone, is the expanded junction where the ventricular body meets the temporal and occipital horns. It contains CSF and a prominent choroid plexus component. Its importance is as a fluid-space junction and anatomical landmark; it is not a separate association center despite its position among temporal, parietal, and occipital structures.",
    "connectionsText": "The atrium communicates directly with the body, inferior horn, and posterior horn. Callosal fibers, including the tapetum, form part of its surrounding white matter, and optic radiations lie nearby. The choroid plexus glomus occupies this region. These relationships explain local surgical constraints and fluid continuity without implying that the ventricular cavity carries neural signals.",
    "clinicalText": "A mass or scar near the atrium can obstruct the temporal horn, causing localized ventricular enlargement and pressure on adjacent tissue. Atrial lesions also pose surgical risks to optic radiations and connected white matter. Normal choroid plexus and ventricular asymmetries must be distinguished from pathology; an atrial shape difference alone does not establish disease.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_ventricular_surgery",
      "lb_subcortex_imaging",
      "lb_trapped_horn"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_basilar_part_of_pons": {
    "functionText": "The basilar, or ventral, pons contains pontine nuclei interwoven with descending and transverse fiber bundles. It combines a relay from cerebral cortex to cerebellum with passage of motor commands toward lower brainstem and spinal cord. This organization distinguishes it from the dorsal pontine tegmentum, which contains many cranial nerve, gaze, and arousal-related systems.",
    "connectionsText": "Corticopontine fibers terminate in pontine nuclei; pontocerebellar axons then cross and enter the opposite middle cerebellar peduncle. Corticospinal and corticobulbar pathways run longitudinally through the region toward spinal and cranial motor systems. These crossing and descending routes allow the pons to participate in coordinated movement while also transmitting commands generated by cerebral networks.",
    "clinicalText": "A unilateral ventral pontine lesion can cause contralateral weakness and sometimes ataxia or dysarthria depending on the affected fibers. Extensive bilateral injury can produce a locked-in state when motor output is severely interrupted while awareness is preserved through spared systems. The precise pattern depends on whether the tegmentum, cranial pathways, or cerebellar connections are also involved.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_pons",
      "lb_pyramidal_lesions",
      "lb_middle_peduncle"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_basolateral_nucleus_basal_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The basal nucleus helps associate sensory events with current value, remembered outcomes, and possible actions. Its neurons participate in both appetitive and aversive learning, including changing a response when circumstances change. In this label, basolateral refers specifically to the basal nucleus; some publications use basolateral amygdala more broadly to include lateral and accessory basal nuclei.",
    "connectionsText": "The basal nucleus receives input from the lateral amygdala and exchanges information with prefrontal, temporal association, and hippocampal regions. Projections to ventral striatum help connect learned significance to motivated action, while interactions with central amygdala influence behavioral and autonomic responses. Subdivisions and cell populations participate in different pathways, rather than forwarding one uniform emotional signal.",
    "clinicalText": "Basal amygdala circuits are investigated in emotional memory, fear learning, and temporal lobe epilepsy. Much mechanistic evidence comes from animal circuit manipulations and cannot be equated with a selective human lesion syndrome. Injury involving the basal nucleus usually also affects neighboring amygdala or temporal structures, making resulting changes in memory or behavior network-dependent.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_basal",
      "lb_reward",
      "lb_amygdala_human"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_basomedial_nucleus_accessory_basal_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The accessory basal, or basomedial, nucleus is part of the deep amygdala group that combines contextual and cortical information with motivational significance. Its organization differs from that of the lateral nucleus, which is a major sensory entry point. Experimental work suggests roles in context-sensitive defensive and approach behavior, with substantial differences across subdivisions and species.",
    "connectionsText": "Connections include hippocampal and parahippocampal territories, prefrontal cortex, other basal amygdala nuclei, and ventral striatum. Primate studies also identify hypothalamic relationships. This combination places the accessory basal nucleus between contextual representations and systems selecting bodily or behavioral responses; detailed cellular pathways established in animals should not be treated as proven human functions of the entire parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Human studies rarely isolate accessory basal damage from the rest of the amygdala. Changes in emotional learning or memory after medial temporal lesions therefore cannot be confidently assigned to this nucleus alone. Its appearance in research on psychiatric conditions indicates a candidate circuit component, not a specific diagnostic marker or a settled lesion-to-symptom relationship.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_diversity",
      "lb_amygdala_hypothalamus",
      "lb_reward"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_bed_nucleus_of_stria_terminalis": {
    "functionText": "The bed nucleus of the stria terminalis contributes to monitoring potential threat and coordinating sustained internal-state responses. It is especially studied during uncertainty and anticipation, and also participates in reward and stress-related behavior. The familiar contrast with brief amygdala responses is useful but incomplete: human evidence shows overlapping functions and responses on multiple time scales.",
    "connectionsText": "It communicates with central amygdala through extended-amygdala pathways, including the stria terminalis and ventral routes. Connections with hypothalamus, periaqueductal gray, brainstem, and ventral striatum link threat monitoring to autonomic, endocrine, and motivational regulation. Its many small subdivisions and cell types can have different or opposing effects, so the atlas groups a diverse circuit.",
    "clinicalText": "Human imaging and animal experiments implicate these circuits in anxiety and stress-related addiction mechanisms. They do not establish that a larger or more active bed nucleus explains an individual’s symptoms. Selective lesions are uncommon, routine imaging has limited resolution here, and clinical effects of nearby basal forebrain injury depend on the additional pathways involved.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_bnst",
      "lb_extended_amygdala",
      "lb_extended_connectivity"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_body_of_hippocampus": {
    "functionText": "The hippocampal body occupies the middle of the long axis and supports relational and episodic memory through its organized subfields. It lies within continuous anterior-to-posterior gradients rather than forming a functionally uniform middle module. Both the information being remembered and the particular dentate, CA, or subicular circuit matter more than the gross body label alone.",
    "connectionsText": "The body receives entorhinal input and contains interacting dentate gyrus, CA3, CA1, and subicular pathways. Its longitudinal connections link anterior and posterior hippocampal territories, while the fimbria carries fibers toward the fornix. Parahippocampal and wider cortical networks provide contextual information and receive hippocampal output during memory encoding, retrieval, and subsequent consolidation.",
    "clinicalText": "Hypoxic injury, seizures, inflammation, or degenerative disease can affect the body and its subfields, producing difficulty learning new information when enough of the memory network is involved. Bilateral damage generally poses greater risk than a small unilateral lesion. Clinical interpretation should examine subfield vulnerability and the full long axis rather than treating the body as an independent memory organ.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_hippocampus_molecular",
      "lb_hippocampus",
      "lb_memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_body_of_lateral_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The ventricular body is the central CSF-containing portion of each lateral ventricle, extending behind the foramen of Monro toward the atrium. Choroid plexus along its medial floor contributes to CSF production. The cavity supports fluid circulation and anatomical separation of adjacent structures; it does not itself integrate sensory information, generate movement, or store memories.",
    "connectionsText": "The corpus callosum forms the roof, while the thalamus and caudate contribute to the floor and lateral boundaries; the septum pellucidum and fornix lie medially. The body is continuous with the anterior horn and atrium, and communicates with the third ventricle through Monro. These connections describe CSF pathways and anatomical neighbors rather than neuronal projections.",
    "clinicalText": "Body enlargement can accompany impaired CSF circulation or loss of surrounding brain tissue. Periventricular signal changes may reflect several different processes and cannot be interpreted from shape alone. Lesions near Monro can obstruct outflow from one or both lateral ventricles, while procedures in this region must account for nearby fornix and deep venous structures.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_subcortex_imaging",
      "lb_lateral_ventricles",
      "lb_hydrocephalus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_central_canal_of_medulla_oblongata": {
    "functionText": "The central canal in the caudal medulla is the small ependymal-lined continuation of the ventricular system toward the spinal cord. It represents a residual fluid channel of the neural tube, with patency varying across individuals and age. It is not a respiratory or autonomic nucleus, and major adult CSF circulation occurs through subarachnoid spaces instead.",
    "connectionsText": "Rostrally the canal is continuous with the narrowing fourth ventricle near the obex; caudally it continues toward the spinal central canal. Surrounding medullary tissue contains sensory, motor, and autonomic circuits. These are adjacent neural structures rather than synaptic connections of the canal, whose defining relationship is continuity of a small potential CSF space.",
    "clinicalText": "Abnormal fluid cavities extending into the medulla are described as syringobulbia and may accompany Chiari-related CSF disturbances. Expansion can injure nearby cranial nerve pathways and produce swallowing, speech, sensory, or breathing problems. Such a cavity is not simply the normal atlas canal enlarged on a screen; diagnosis depends on clinical imaging and the surrounding structural abnormalities.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_ventricles_neuroscience",
      "lb_syringobulbia",
      "lb_csf"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_central_nuclear_group": {
    "functionText": "This label denotes the central group of amygdala nuclei, a major interface between learned significance and defensive, autonomic, and motivational responses. Its inhibitory microcircuits select and regulate outputs rather than simply relaying a fear command. Different neuronal populations contribute to feeding, attention, reinforcement, and responses to threat, with much detailed causal evidence coming from animals.",
    "connectionsText": "Inputs arrive from basal and lateral amygdala circuits, insular and other cortical regions, and visceral-related pathways. Outputs reach hypothalamus, periaqueductal gray, parabrachial and other brainstem regions. Connections with the bed nucleus of the stria terminalis form part of the central extended amygdala, coordinating responses whose duration and form depend on context.",
    "clinicalText": "Central amygdala dysfunction is studied in threat responding and stress-related disorders, but a selective human central-nucleus syndrome is not well defined. Human lesions or resections typically cross several amygdala subdivisions. Changes in startle, autonomic responses, or emotional learning therefore require physiological and behavioral evidence rather than inference from the atlas boundary alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_diversity",
      "lb_extended_amygdala",
      "lb_extended_connectivity"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cerebellar_deep_nuclei": {
    "functionText": "The deep cerebellar nuclei are major output structures of the cerebellum, integrating inhibitory Purkinje-cell signals with incoming excitatory information. Dentate, interposed, and fastigial nuclei participate in different loops for movement timing, coordination, posture, and aspects of cognition. They help translate cerebellar processing into changes in other motor and association systems rather than directly contracting muscles.",
    "connectionsText": "Dentate output primarily travels through the superior cerebellar peduncle toward contralateral thalamic and red-nucleus pathways. Interposed nuclei participate in limb-control loops, while fastigial connections emphasize vestibular and reticular systems for axial and balance control. The nuclei also receive collaterals of incoming cerebellar pathways, allowing comparison of afferent information with the processed output of cerebellar cortex.",
    "clinicalText": "Deep nuclear injury can produce substantial ataxia, dysmetria, and impaired coordination, with deficits depending on which nucleus and output fibers are affected. Damage to dentate-related circuits can also contribute to cognitive or speech disturbances. These nuclei lie close to one another, so clinical lesions often disrupt several loops and cannot be interpreted as a single uniform cerebellar deficit.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_dentate",
      "lb_cerebellum_atlas",
      "lb_cerebellum_physiology"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cerebellar_vermis": {
    "functionText": "The vermis is the midline cerebellar region important for coordinating axial posture, gait, and eye movements. Different lobules participate in different networks, and posterior vermal regions also contribute to affective regulation. Its midline position should therefore not be taken to mean one simple function, but balance and control of the body’s central axis are prominent clinical themes.",
    "connectionsText": "Vermal Purkinje cells influence fastigial and vestibular-related circuits. Inputs include spinal, vestibular, and brainstem information about body position and motion, while output pathways reach vestibular nuclei and reticular formation to adjust posture and gaze. These circuits cooperate with neighboring intermediate cerebellum and bilateral descending systems rather than controlling one isolated muscle group.",
    "clinicalText": "Vermal lesions often cause truncal instability and a broad-based, unsteady gait, sometimes with abnormal eye movements. The exact findings depend on lobule and neighboring vestibulocerebellar involvement. Developmental abnormalities, tumors, degeneration, or stroke can affect this region; limb strength may remain relatively preserved because the primary problem is coordination and stability rather than loss of motor command.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_cerebellum_organization",
      "lb_cerebellum",
      "lb_cerebellum_physiology"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cerebral_aqueduct": {
    "functionText": "The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow CSF channel through the midbrain connecting the third and fourth ventricles. Its small caliber makes patency especially important for ventricular circulation. The channel is lined by ependyma and does not contain a choroid plexus or perform the pain, autonomic, or eye-movement functions of neighboring midbrain tissue.",
    "connectionsText": "Superiorly the aqueduct opens into the third ventricle and inferiorly into the fourth. Periaqueductal gray surrounds it, while tectal and tegmental structures lie nearby. Its relevant connection is a fluid pathway through the midbrain; proximity to neural centers explains why an obstructing lesion can combine hydrocephalus with additional local neurological signs.",
    "clinicalText": "Aqueductal narrowing or obstruction can produce enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles while the fourth remains relatively normal. Causes include developmental stenosis, scarring, and compression by nearby lesions. Imaging evaluates both the channel and the surrounding midbrain; a visible narrowing alone does not establish its functional severity without the ventricular pattern and clinical context.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_aqueduct",
      "lb_csf"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_cerebral_peduncle_crus_cerebri": {
    "functionText": "The crus cerebri is the ventral fiber-rich part of the cerebral peduncle. It carries major descending pathways from cerebral cortex toward the pons, cranial motor systems, and spinal cord. The atlas label refers to this motor and corticopontine conduit rather than the whole midbrain peduncular region, which also includes neighboring gray matter and other pathways.",
    "connectionsText": "Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers descend from the internal capsule through the crus, while corticopontine fibers connect cortical territories with pontine nuclei. Farther caudally, motor fibers continue through the pons toward medullary and spinal pathways. The substantia nigra lies just dorsal to the crus, so close anatomical proximity should not be confused with identical function.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage can cause contralateral weakness because corticospinal fibers have not yet crossed at the medullary decussation. A larger ventral midbrain lesion involving nearby oculomotor fibers can combine same-side third-nerve palsy with opposite-side weakness. This combined pattern reflects adjacent structures, and an isolated crus lesion need not produce the complete named brainstem syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_internal_capsule",
      "lb_pyramidal",
      "lb_midbrain"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_corpus_callosum": {
    "functionText": "The corpus callosum is the largest commissural bundle linking the cerebral hemispheres. It supports integration and coordination of sensory, motor, and cognitive activity, including both cooperative and inhibitory interactions. Its regions carry different cortical connections, so the functional consequence of a lesion depends on which fibers are interrupted rather than on the callosum as one undifferentiated bridge.",
    "connectionsText": "Anterior portions link frontal territories, the body carries substantial motor and somatosensory connections, and posterior regions including the splenium link posterior cortical areas. Fibers fan into each hemisphere rather than connecting every point symmetrically. The anterior and hippocampal commissures provide additional interhemispheric routes but do not simply replace the full range of callosal connections.",
    "clinicalText": "Acquired callosal disconnection can disrupt transfer of information between hemispheres, producing findings such as impaired cross-hand coordination or difficulty naming stimuli available primarily to the nondominant hemisphere. Developmental absence has a wide range of outcomes because networks develop differently. Demyelination, trauma, infarction, and other pathology can affect the bundle, with symptoms depending on location and associated injury.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_callosum",
      "lb_commissures"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_dorsal_lateral_geniculate_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus is the principal thalamic relay of the retina-to-visual-cortex pathway. It preserves an organized map of the opposite visual field while separating and modulating streams of visual information. Retinal input is strongly influenced by cortical and state-related feedback, so the nucleus actively regulates transmission rather than serving as a passive cable junction.",
    "connectionsText": "Each nucleus receives the opposite visual hemifield from both eyes through the optic tract: crossed nasal-retinal and uncrossed temporal-retinal fibers. Output travels through the optic radiations to primary visual cortex. Feedback from visual cortex and thalamic reticular circuits adjusts processing, while its layered organization helps maintain distinct retinal information channels.",
    "clinicalText": "A unilateral lesion can produce a homonymous visual-field defect on the opposite side, sometimes with a sector-shaped pattern depending on the affected territory. Such injury is different from monocular optic nerve loss or bitemporal chiasmal loss. Clinical field testing and imaging are needed because nearby optic tract, radiation, and cortical damage can produce related patterns.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_visual_primate",
      "lb_visual_pathway",
      "lb_thalamic_nuclei"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_fornix": {
    "functionText": "The fornix is a major white matter pathway of the hippocampal formation, supporting communication within memory circuits rather than storing memories as a discrete center. Its fibers carry hippocampal and subicular output and also include incoming modulatory connections. The curved bundle links medial temporal structures with the basal forebrain and diencephalon beneath the corpus callosum.",
    "connectionsText": "Fibers from the alveus collect into the fimbria, crura, body, and columns of the fornix. Postcommissural fibers reach mammillary bodies and other diencephalic targets; precommissural fibers connect with septal and basal forebrain regions. Through mammillary and anterior thalamic circuits, hippocampal information participates in a broader network that also includes cingulate and retrosplenial cortex.",
    "clinicalText": "Bilateral fornix damage can cause marked difficulty forming or retrieving episodic memories, sometimes with relatively preserved general intellectual abilities. Infarction, tumors near the third ventricle, and surgery can injure its closely packed fibers. Unilateral effects are more variable, and the outcome depends on other memory-network structures, so lesion location matters more than a generic white matter label.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_fornix",
      "lb_stroke_memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_fourth_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The fourth ventricle is the CSF-containing cavity between the dorsal pons and upper medulla and the cerebellum. It receives fluid through the aqueduct and provides the main ventricular exits into the subarachnoid space. Its floor overlies important brainstem nuclei, but the ventricular space itself does not control breathing, balance, swallowing, or eye movements.",
    "connectionsText": "The aqueduct enters superiorly; paired lateral apertures of Luschka and the median aperture of Magendie connect to surrounding CSF spaces. Caudally it narrows toward the central canal. The cerebellar roof and brainstem floor are anatomical boundaries, while choroid plexus contributes CSF. Distinguishing these relationships from neural connections prevents attributing neighboring brainstem functions to the cavity.",
    "clinicalText": "Posterior fossa tumors, hemorrhage, or outlet scarring can obstruct this compartment and cause hydrocephalus. Expansion or a nearby mass may also compress the cerebellum and brainstem. Clinical effects can therefore combine raised-pressure symptoms with cranial nerve or coordination findings, and procedures must account for the vulnerable neural structures immediately beneath the ventricular floor.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_ventricles",
      "lb_csf",
      "lb_ventricles_neuroscience"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_head_of_hippocampus": {
    "functionText": "The hippocampal head is the expanded anterior portion of a structure essential for forming and relating episodic memories. Along a gradual longitudinal organization, anterior regions are often associated with broader contextual representations and interactions with emotion and motivation. This is a tendency across networks, not a separate memory system or a sharp division from the body.",
    "connectionsText": "Entorhinal inputs reach hippocampal circuits through pathways involving dentate gyrus and CA fields, with output through CA1 and subiculum. Anterior hippocampal regions have relatively strong relationships with amygdala and medial prefrontal networks. The fimbria and fornix connect the formation to septal and diencephalic structures; these connections vary continuously along the long axis.",
    "clinicalText": "The head is a common focus of assessment in mesial temporal epilepsy and hippocampal sclerosis, although disease may extend farther posteriorly. Injury can impair new episodic learning, with language dominance and the opposite hippocampus affecting the pattern. A head-volume change alone cannot specify memory ability, and anterior specialization should not be mistaken for an isolated clinical syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_hippocampus_gradients",
      "lb_amygdala_memory",
      "lb_hippocampus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_cerebellar_peduncle": {
    "functionText": "The inferior cerebellar peduncle is a mixed pathway linking the cerebellum with medulla, spinal cord, and vestibular systems. Much of its traffic carries sensory and teaching information needed for balance and coordination. It includes restiform and juxtarestiform components with different connections, so the entire peduncle should not be described as a single one-way sensory tract.",
    "connectionsText": "Major inputs include dorsal spinocerebellar and cuneocerebellar pathways, olivocerebellar climbing fibers, and vestibular connections. The juxtarestiform region also carries cerebellar output toward vestibular and reticular nuclei. These routes supply body-state information and coordinate posture, gaze, and learned movement adjustments, complementing the cortical input carried by the middle peduncle and major output carried by the superior peduncle.",
    "clinicalText": "Injury may cause ipsilateral ataxia, imbalance, and ocular motor or vestibular symptoms. In lateral medullary infarction it is often damaged together with nearby vestibular, sensory, and swallowing pathways, producing a mixed syndrome. The presence of vertigo alone does not localize a lesion here, and clinical interpretation must consider the accompanying brainstem signs.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_cerebellum",
      "lb_cerebellum_physiology",
      "lb_olive"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_colliculus": {
    "functionText": "The inferior colliculus is a major midbrain integration center for auditory information. Its subdivisions combine signals related to frequency, timing, and binaural cues before transmission to the auditory thalamus. It is distinct from the superior colliculus’s prominent orienting role, although the two interact when sound helps direct attention or movements toward a source.",
    "connectionsText": "Inputs converge from cochlear-nuclear, superior-olivary, and lateral-lemniscal pathways, carrying information originating from both ears. The central nucleus has an organized frequency map and projects strongly through the brachium to medial geniculate nuclei. Connections with auditory cortex, the opposite colliculus, and other midbrain systems provide feedback and links between acoustic processing and behavioral orientation.",
    "clinicalText": "A unilateral lesion can disturb sound localization or other auditory processing without causing complete deafness because pathways are already bilateral. Bilateral inferior-collicular damage can produce profound central hearing impairment. Clinical findings depend on subdivision and nearby brainstem involvement, and hearing thresholds alone may not capture difficulties with timing, localization, or understanding complex acoustic environments.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_inferior_colliculus",
      "lb_auditory_pathways"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_horn_of_lateral_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The inferior, or temporal, horn curves into the temporal lobe alongside the hippocampus. It contains CSF and choroid plexus and is part of the lateral ventricular system. Its close relationship to memory structures makes it a useful imaging landmark, but the horn itself is a fluid compartment rather than tissue that performs learning or emotional processing.",
    "connectionsText": "The temporal horn continues from the atrium and ends anteriorly near the amygdala. The hippocampus forms a major part of its floor, while the caudate tail and tapetal white matter contribute to surrounding boundaries. Choroidal structures lie medially and optic radiations run nearby, linking ventricular anatomy to both medial temporal and visual surgical considerations.",
    "clinicalText": "Temporal horn enlargement can occur in hydrocephalus or alongside hippocampal tissue loss; these mechanisms require different interpretations. Obstruction near the atrium can trap the horn while local choroid plexus continues producing fluid, causing progressive expansion. Resulting symptoms reflect pressure on surrounding tissue, and the distinction depends on the full ventricular pattern and clinical context.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_trapped_horn",
      "lb_hydrocephalus",
      "lb_ventricular_surgery"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_inferior_olive": {
    "functionText": "The inferior olivary complex in the medulla supplies climbing-fiber signals to the cerebellum. These signals contribute to the timing, coordination, and plasticity of cerebellar processing, including learning from movement-related events. The olive should not be confused with the superior olivary auditory complex: its main role is in olivocerebellar circuitry, with several anatomically organized subnuclei.",
    "connectionsText": "Olivary neurons receive signals from spinal, brainstem, and descending motor-related pathways. Their axons cross the midline and enter the opposite cerebellum through the inferior peduncle, contacting Purkinje cells as climbing fibers and providing collaterals to deep nuclei. Feedback from deep cerebellar nuclei helps regulate olivary activity within recurrent coordination and learning circuits.",
    "clinicalText": "Interruption of pathways in the dentato-rubro-olivary network can lead to hypertrophic olivary degeneration, sometimes associated with palatal or ocular tremor. This is a transsynaptic response and need not mean the original injury occurred in the olive itself. Other degenerative conditions involve olivocerebellar systems more broadly, with symptoms depending on the accompanying cerebellar and brainstem damage.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_olive",
      "lb_red_nucleus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_ingulo_parahippocampal_isthmus": {
    "functionText": "The cingulo-parahippocampal isthmus is the narrow cortical transition behind and below the splenium where the cingulate region continues toward the parahippocampal gyrus. It lies within networks supporting contextual memory and orientation. The surface landmark is not identical to the underlying cingulum bundle, and neither should be assigned a single, isolated psychological function.",
    "connectionsText": "Nearby retrosplenial and posterior cingulate territories exchange information with parahippocampal and entorhinal cortex, hippocampal systems, and anterior thalamic networks. The underlying isthmic cingulum carries part of this communication and intersects other white matter routes. Cortical continuity and tract proximity are helpful for orientation but do not imply that every neighboring structure connects directly through this parcel.",
    "clinicalText": "Posteromedial lesions can cause disorientation or memory difficulty by disrupting retrosplenial and medial temporal networks. Surgical approaches around the splenium must consider the cingulum and nearby visual pathways. Evidence for a unique syndrome of the narrow isthmus itself is limited, so effects should be attributed to documented cortical and white matter involvement rather than the landmark alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_cingulum",
      "lb_isthmus",
      "lb_stroke_memory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_hemisphere_of_cerebellum": {
    "functionText": "The lateral cerebellar hemisphere participates in planning and timing complex actions and in loops with cerebral association cortex. Some territories support language, executive, and other cognitive operations as well as movement. This broad region contains multiple functional maps, so describing it only as a balance center misses its extensive contribution to predictive and coordinated processing.",
    "connectionsText": "Cerebral cortex sends information through pontine nuclei and the middle cerebellar peduncle to lateral cerebellar circuits. Purkinje output strongly influences dentate nuclei, whose fibers return through superior-peduncle and thalamic pathways to motor and association cortex. These reciprocal loops allow cerebellar computations to refine cortical activity without making the hemisphere a direct descending motor-command pathway.",
    "clinicalText": "Lateral hemisphere lesions can cause ipsilateral limb incoordination and impaired movement timing; posterior association territories may contribute to cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome. Effects vary with lobular location and dentate-pathway involvement. A broad atlas parcel cannot predict a person’s language or executive profile, and motor signs should be distinguished from weakness caused by corticospinal injury elsewhere.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_cerebellum",
      "lb_cerebellum_atlas",
      "lb_dentate"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_lateral_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "Here the lateral nucleus is the lateral amygdala nucleus, an important entry region for sensory information used in associative learning. Its circuits help relate cues to consequential outcomes, including both danger and reward. This is a specific amygdala subdivision, not a generic lateral thalamic nucleus, and its role depends on interactions with the rest of the complex.",
    "connectionsText": "Sensory thalamic and cortical pathways supply the lateral nucleus, with association inputs carrying increasingly processed information. It communicates strongly with basal and accessory basal amygdala and with inhibitory intercalated circuits that influence central-amygdala output. These connections allow a sensory cue to acquire significance while context and prior experience shape the resulting response.",
    "clinicalText": "Lateral amygdala plasticity is a central experimental model of learned threat associations, but animal conditioning results do not define a unique human psychiatric diagnosis. Selective human lesions are unusual, and broader amygdala or temporal damage can alter recognition, learning, and memory in different combinations. Clinical localization must distinguish this nucleus from adjacent cortex and other nuclei.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_basal",
      "lb_amygdala_emotion",
      "lb_amygdala_atlas"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_medial_geniculate_nuclei": {
    "functionText": "The medial geniculate nuclei form the auditory thalamus, processing and routing sound information toward cortex. The ventral division emphasizes organized frequency-related transmission, while dorsal and medial divisions participate in broader auditory and multisensory interactions. These nuclei help shape responses to acoustic features and behavioral context; hearing is not represented by one undifferentiated relay.",
    "connectionsText": "The inferior colliculus supplies major ascending input through its brachium, with the ventral division linking strongly to primary auditory cortex through auditory radiations. Other divisions communicate with auditory association and additional cortical networks. Extensive corticothalamic feedback makes these recurrent circuits, allowing ongoing cortical activity and behavioral demands to influence the transmission of sound information.",
    "clinicalText": "A unilateral lesion more often disrupts complex auditory processing than causes complete deafness because ascending auditory pathways carry information from both ears. Bilateral damage can produce much more severe hearing deficits. Difficulties with sound localization or interpretation require careful assessment, and tinnitus or language problems alone do not specifically identify injury to the medial geniculate nuclei.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_auditory_thalamus",
      "lb_auditory_pathways",
      "lb_thalamic_radiations"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_medial_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The medial amygdala nucleus participates in circuits relating chemosensory information to internal state and social behavior. Its functions are especially well characterized in animals with prominent accessory olfactory systems. Human olfactory studies support a role in odor-related networks, but assigning rodent mating, aggression, or pheromone functions directly to this human atlas region would be unjustified.",
    "connectionsText": "The medial nucleus connects with other olfactory amygdala territories and with hypothalamic and bed-nucleus circuits. Human imaging supports a relationship with the olfactory bulb, while comparative studies provide more detailed pathway information. These routes link sensory cues to physiological and motivational regulation, although tractography alone cannot demonstrate a specific behavioral computation or the direction of signaling.",
    "clinicalText": "There is no reliable, isolated medial-amygdala lesion syndrome in humans. Research associations with social or affective processing remain broader than a diagnostic claim about this small nucleus. Disease involving the medial temporal lobe may affect olfaction and emotional behavior through several adjacent structures, so species limits, imaging resolution, and lesion extent are essential to interpretation.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_olfactory",
      "lb_amygdala_olfactory_network",
      "lb_amygdala_diversity"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_midbrain_tegmentum": {
    "functionText": "The midbrain tegmentum is a broad central region containing nuclei and pathways involved in movement, arousal, eye control, and sensory transmission. It is not one functional nucleus. Its exact contribution depends on location: red-nucleus and cerebellar circuits, reticular systems, ocular motor pathways, and ascending sensory tracts occupy different parts of this compact territory.",
    "connectionsText": "Connections include ascending medial lemniscal and other sensory pathways, superior cerebellar peduncle fibers, reticular projections, and ocular motor circuits associated with the medial longitudinal fasciculus. Dopaminergic ventral tegmental territories and red-nucleus networks add motivational and motor relationships. Because atlas boundaries group several systems, selecting the region should prompt inspection of its component pathways and neighbors.",
    "clinicalText": "Small midbrain lesions can produce combinations of eye-movement abnormalities, ataxia, tremor, or sensory changes, while more extensive bilateral involvement may impair alertness. Different combinations reflect different nuclei and passing fibers. Clinical localization therefore depends on the pattern and side of findings rather than assuming that all tegmental injury produces one standard syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_midbrain",
      "lb_brainstem",
      "lb_red_nucleus"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_middle_cerebellar_peduncle": {
    "functionText": "The middle cerebellar peduncle is the large bundle carrying pontine input into the cerebellum. It provides a major route by which cerebral cortical activity reaches cerebellar circuits for coordination, timing, and prediction. Its function is principally afferent, distinguishing it from the superior peduncle’s prominent output role and the inferior peduncle’s mixed sensory and vestibular connections.",
    "connectionsText": "Corticopontine fibers terminate in pontine nuclei, whose axons cross the midline and enter the opposite cerebellar hemisphere through this peduncle. Thus, each peduncle carries information that originated largely in the contralateral cerebral cortex. The pathway supplies mossy-fiber input to cerebellar circuits and links motor and association cortical networks with their corresponding cerebellar processing loops.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions can produce ipsilateral cerebellar incoordination, gait difficulty, and dysarthria. Bilateral signal abnormalities occur in several degenerative, inflammatory, metabolic, and vascular conditions, so an imaging pattern is not diagnostic by itself. Nearby pontine involvement may add facial, sensory, or gaze findings, and distinguishing primary peduncular disease from degeneration of incoming fibers is clinically useful.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_middle_peduncle",
      "lb_cerebellum_organization"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_midline_nuclear_complex": {
    "functionText": "The thalamic midline nuclear complex includes small nuclei such as reuniens, rhomboid, paraventricular, and paratenial nuclei. These participate in memory, motivation, arousal, and internal-state regulation through different circuits. The label groups heterogeneous nuclei rather than a single computation, and the thalamic paraventricular nucleus should not be confused with the hypothalamic nucleus of the same name.",
    "connectionsText": "Reuniens and rhomboid circuits link medial prefrontal and hippocampal systems, while paraventricular and paratenial territories interact with amygdala, ventral striatum, and other limbic regions. Inputs from hypothalamic and brainstem systems convey state-related information. Many detailed pathways are established mainly through animal tracing and experimental manipulation, with human imaging providing a less fine-grained picture.",
    "clinicalText": "Paramedian thalamic injury can disturb memory, alertness, or behavior, but usually affects more than the midline nuclei alone. Human evidence rarely isolates one of these tiny nuclei, making nucleus-specific clinical claims uncertain. The region’s role in experimental memory or stress circuits is informative without implying that its shape or activity establishes an individual cognitive or psychiatric diagnosis.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_midline",
      "lb_reuniens",
      "lb_thalamus_cognition"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_olfactory_bulb": {
    "functionText": "The olfactory bulb is the first major central processing stage for odor signals from the nasal olfactory epithelium. Inputs converge in glomeruli, where local circuits shape activity in mitral and tufted cells. The bulb organizes and refines patterns of sensory input while receiving feedback, so odor perception emerges from an active network rather than a simple chemical detector.",
    "connectionsText": "Olfactory receptor axons pass through the cribriform plate and contact bulb circuits. Mitral and tufted cell axons travel in the olfactory tract to anterior olfactory nucleus, piriform cortex, and other primary olfactory regions. Interneurons provide local inhibition, while centrifugal connections from olfactory cortex and neuromodulatory systems adjust processing according to experience and state.",
    "clinicalText": "Head trauma can shear olfactory axons near the cribriform plate, and bulb or tract injury can contribute to reduced smell. Inflammatory, developmental, and degenerative conditions may also alter bulb anatomy. Smell loss is therefore not specific to a brain disorder, and bulb volume alone cannot distinguish peripheral receptor damage from disrupted central processing.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_olfactory_nerve",
      "lb_olfactory_wiring"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_olfactory_tract": {
    "functionText": "The olfactory tract carries patterned output from the bulb toward primary olfactory regions. It is a white matter pathway, not a separate odor-identification center. Unlike most sensory systems, initial olfactory cortical access does not require a thalamic relay, although later olfactory processing involves extensive thalamic, frontal, limbic, and memory-related interactions.",
    "connectionsText": "Mitral and tufted cell axons form major tract components, reaching the anterior olfactory nucleus and, through lateral olfactory routes, piriform cortex and olfactory amygdala territories. Further connections engage entorhinal and orbitofrontal networks. Feedback and commissural pathways contribute to this system, so an anatomical tract view represents only part of the recurrent olfactory circuit.",
    "clinicalText": "Compression, trauma, or surgery along the inferior frontal surface can interrupt odor transmission through the tract. Unilateral injury may be less obvious without testing each nostril separately, and odor identification also depends on intact cortical processing. Assessment must consider nasal and bulb causes as well as the tract; smell impairment alone does not precisely localize a lesion.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_olfactory_memory",
      "lb_olfactory_nerve",
      "lb_olfactory_wiring"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_optic_radiation": {
    "functionText": "The optic radiation is the white matter pathway carrying geniculate visual signals to primary visual cortex. Its fibers preserve the arrangement of the opposite visual field while traveling through temporal and parietal white matter. Different parts carry different field sectors, making its course important for interpreting visual-field loss and planning surgery around the lateral ventricle.",
    "connectionsText": "Fibers originate in the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus and terminate around the calcarine sulcus. Inferior retinal fibers representing the upper visual field sweep anteriorly into temporal lobe as Meyer’s loop; superior retinal fibers representing the lower field follow a more parietal route. Nearby ventricular horns are landmarks, not parts of the neural visual pathway.",
    "clinicalText": "Temporal injury affecting Meyer’s loop can cause a contralateral superior quadrantanopia, while parietal involvement may affect the inferior quadrant. More extensive radiation damage can produce homonymous hemianopia. Individual pathway variation matters, particularly during temporal surgery, and visual-field testing is needed because a schematic tract outline cannot predict the exact deficit in a particular brain.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_visual_pathway",
      "lb_thalamic_radiations",
      "lb_ventricular_surgery"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_optic_tract": {
    "functionText": "The optic tract carries postchiasmal retinal information toward central visual targets. Each tract represents the opposite half of visual space using fibers from both eyes, rather than carrying all vision from one eye. This organization is fundamental to recognizing homonymous field loss and distinguishing a tract lesion from damage to the optic nerve before the chiasm.",
    "connectionsText": "The tract contains crossed axons from the opposite nasal retina and uncrossed axons from the same-side temporal retina. Most fibers continue to the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus, while other retinal projections reach pretectal and superior-collicular pathways for reflexive responses and orienting. These branches distinguish image-forming vision from additional light-dependent and visuomotor functions.",
    "clinicalText": "Unilateral tract damage typically causes a contralateral homonymous visual-field deficit, often less congruent between the eyes than a posterior cortical lesion. A relative afferent pupillary abnormality may accompany the field loss. Compression, inflammation, vascular injury, or trauma can affect the tract, but localization requires examination because neighboring chiasmal and geniculate involvement changes the pattern.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_optic_tract",
      "lb_visual_pathway"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_paravermis_of_cerebellum": {
    "functionText": "The paravermis, or intermediate cerebellar zone, lies beside the vermis and contributes strongly to ongoing limb coordination. It helps adjust movement using information about intended action and actual limb state. This emphasis differs from the vermis’s axial and balance functions and the more lateral hemisphere’s planning-related loops, although the zones interact continuously.",
    "connectionsText": "Intermediate-zone Purkinje cells project chiefly to the interposed nuclei. Spinocerebellar and other inputs convey proprioceptive and movement-related information, while interposed output reaches red-nucleus and thalamocortical pathways through the superior cerebellar peduncle. These loops help refine limb trajectories and timing, with somatotopic representations distributed across more than one cerebellar region.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage can produce ipsilateral limb dysmetria, intention tremor, and poorly timed alternating movements. This differs from pure weakness: the limb may generate force but fail to reach a target smoothly. Clinical lesions rarely respect the entire functional-zone boundary, so associated gait, speech, or eye-movement findings depend on spread to vermis, deep nuclei, or neighboring cerebellar pathways.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_cerebellum_organization",
      "lb_cerebellum_atlas",
      "lb_cerebellum_physiology"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_pontine_tegmentum": {
    "functionText": "The pontine tegmentum is the dorsal pons, containing cranial nerve circuits, reticular networks, and long pathways important for sensation, gaze, sleep, and arousal. It differs from the ventral basilar pons’s emphasis on corticopontine and descending motor traffic. The broad atlas region groups several systems whose functions cannot be reduced to one pontine control center.",
    "connectionsText": "Abducens and facial pathways lie near vestibular and auditory-related territories at caudal levels, while trigeminal systems occupy more rostral and lateral regions. The medial longitudinal fasciculus links ocular motor nuclei, ascending sensory tracts continue toward thalamus, and reticular connections reach forebrain and spinal systems. These tightly packed routes explain the diversity of pontine signs.",
    "clinicalText": "Tegmental lesions can cause horizontal gaze abnormalities, facial weakness, altered facial sensation, ataxia, or other crossed brainstem findings. Larger lesions affecting reticular systems may impair alertness. The clinical pattern depends on level and extent; preserved consciousness after a purely ventral pontine injury should not be generalized to injury involving the dorsal tegmentum.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_pons",
      "lb_brainstem",
      "lb_trigeminal"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_posterior_cortical_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The posterior cortical nucleus belongs to the superficial, cortex-like portion of the amygdala and is associated with olfactory and limbic integration. Its name covers a territory whose subdivisions and homologies vary across atlases and species. A detailed human behavioral function is less certain than the general role of cortical amygdala in relating odor information to significance.",
    "connectionsText": "Animal tracing describes interactions with other cortical and medial amygdala regions, amygdalohippocampal territory, and olfactory networks. These results should be distinguished from human findings: recent bulb tractography does not give the posterior cortical nucleus the same strong connectivity pattern seen in anterior cortical and medial regions. Neither result establishes all synaptic inputs or a complete human pathway map.",
    "clinicalText": "Isolated posterior cortical nucleus injury is rarely documented, so a unique clinical syndrome is not established. Posterior amygdala involvement in seizures or medial temporal lesions may affect odor-associated memory and emotional responses through a larger network. Its small size and variable boundaries make confident symptom assignment from routine structural imaging particularly difficult.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_amygdala_feedback",
      "lb_amygdalohippocampal",
      "lb_amygdala_olfactory"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_posterior_horn_of_lateral_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The posterior, or occipital, horn is the CSF-filled extension of the lateral ventricle toward the occipital lobe. Its size and shape are notably variable, including marked normal asymmetry. It is a ventricular recess rather than visual cortex, so it neither detects light nor constructs images even though visual pathways and cortex are close to it.",
    "connectionsText": "It extends backward from the atrium and is bordered by callosal white matter, including tapetal fibers, with medial impressions related to the calcarine region. The optic radiations pass in surrounding white matter on their route toward visual cortex. Its only direct continuity is with other ventricular CSF spaces, not with retinal or cortical neural signaling pathways.",
    "clinicalText": "Occipital horn enlargement may reflect global ventricular expansion or local tissue loss, but a small or asymmetric horn can be normal. Surgery or lesions around this recess can affect adjacent visual pathways and produce visual-field deficits. Those deficits arise from neural injury beside the cavity, not from loss of a processing function within the CSF space.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_lateral_ventricles",
      "lb_ventricular_surgery",
      "lb_csf_disorders"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_pretectal_region": {
    "functionText": "The pretectal region lies near the junction of diencephalon and dorsal midbrain, just rostral to the superior colliculus. Its nuclei participate in visual reflexes, especially adjustment of pupil size to light, with other components contributing to motion-related and visuomotor processing. It is distinct from both image-forming visual cortex and the nearby superior-collicular orienting system.",
    "connectionsText": "Retinal signals reach pretectal nuclei through optic pathways. The olivary pretectal circuit projects to bilateral Edinger–Westphal-related parasympathetic pathways, allowing light in one eye to constrict both pupils. Connections crossing near the posterior commissure are important clinically. Other pretectal nuclei have different thalamic and brainstem relationships, so the whole region is broader than the pupil reflex alone.",
    "clinicalText": "Dorsal midbrain compression or injury can disrupt light responses while sparing near responses, producing light-near dissociation. Vertical gaze impairment and convergence-retraction movements may accompany it when nearby pathways are involved in Parinaud syndrome. These combined findings reflect regional anatomy rather than one pretectal nucleus, and a pupil abnormality has other possible peripheral and central causes.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_pretectum",
      "lb_parinaud",
      "lb_optic_tract"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_pyramidal_part_of_medulla_oblongata": {
    "functionText": "The medullary pyramids are ventral bundles carrying descending corticospinal fibers toward the spinal cord. They support voluntary movement, particularly skilled control, by transmitting cortical influence to spinal motor circuits. They are white matter pathways rather than motor nuclei, and their position above the major pyramidal crossing explains the opposite-side weakness caused by many rostral lesions.",
    "connectionsText": "Fibers descend from cerebral cortex through internal capsule, crus cerebri, and ventral pons into the pyramids. Most cross in the caudal medulla to form the lateral corticospinal tract, while a smaller component follows other spinal courses. Connections ultimately influence spinal interneurons and motor neurons; many corticobulbar fibers have already left the descending pathway at higher levels.",
    "clinicalText": "A lesion above the decussation typically produces contralateral upper motor neuron weakness, with later hyperreflexia and abnormal plantar responses. Medial medullary injury may additionally affect nearby medial lemniscal or hypoglossal structures, causing sensory or tongue findings. Those associated signs reflect adjacent anatomy, and damage at or below the crossing changes the expected side of weakness.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_pyramidal",
      "lb_pyramidal_lesions",
      "lb_medulla"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_red_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The red nucleus is a midbrain structure participating in cerebellar and motor networks. Its magnocellular and parvocellular parts have different connections, and their relative importance differs across species. In humans, the prominent parvocellular component is closely related to cerebellar learning circuits; it should not be described simply as the dominant controller of limb movement.",
    "connectionsText": "The nucleus receives cerebellar output through superior-peduncle pathways and input from motor-related cortex. Magnocellular neurons contribute to the crossed rubrospinal pathway, which is less prominent in humans than in many other mammals. Parvocellular connections with the inferior olive form part of a circuit linking cerebral and cerebellar processing through olivocerebellar pathways.",
    "clinicalText": "Lesions around the red nucleus may combine ataxia or tremor with nearby oculomotor and other midbrain signs. Such syndromes often reflect injury to passing cerebellar fibers as well as the nucleus. Disruption of the broader dentato-rubro-olivary circuit can produce delayed olivary changes, so the clinical pattern should be interpreted at the network level.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_red_nucleus",
      "lb_dentate"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_superior_cerebellar_peduncle_brachium_conjunctivum": {
    "functionText": "The superior cerebellar peduncle, or brachium conjunctivum, is the major output route from dentate and interposed cerebellar nuclei toward the midbrain and thalamus. It helps cerebellar processing influence movement planning and execution. It is not exclusively outgoing: important afferent fibers, including much of the ventral spinocerebellar pathway, also travel through it.",
    "connectionsText": "Most major cerebellar output fibers cross in the caudal midbrain and continue toward red nucleus and thalamic targets that communicate with motor and association cortex. The crossing is essential for understanding laterality. Incoming spinocerebellar fibers carry information about spinal motor circuitry, complementing the feedback and cortical inputs arriving through the other peduncles.",
    "clinicalText": "Damage can produce ataxia and dysmetria by disconnecting cerebellar output. Lesions before versus after the decussation affect laterality differently, so nearby midbrain anatomy matters when interpreting signs. More extensive lesions may also involve eye-movement pathways or red-nucleus circuits, producing a combination that should not be attributed to the peduncle alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_dentate",
      "lb_cerebellum_atlas",
      "lb_cerebellum_physiology"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_superior_colliculus": {
    "functionText": "The superior colliculus helps select and orient toward behaviorally relevant locations, coordinating rapid eye and head movements. Its superficial layers emphasize visual input, while deeper layers integrate additional sensory and motor-related information. It contributes to attention and orienting rather than detailed conscious object recognition, which relies heavily on geniculate and cortical visual pathways.",
    "connectionsText": "Retinal and visual-cortical inputs reach superficial layers, while deeper circuits receive auditory, somatosensory, and frontal-eye-field influences. Outputs engage brainstem gaze and head-orienting systems and communicate with thalamic pathways such as the pulvinar. Basal ganglia input through substantia nigra helps regulate selection of orienting responses, connecting sensory priorities with action control.",
    "clinicalText": "Collicular or nearby dorsal midbrain injury may impair saccadic orienting and visual attention. Larger lesions can also disturb vertical gaze or pupil responses by involving adjacent pretectal and ocular motor pathways. Those neighboring signs should not all be attributed to the superior colliculus itself, and detailed examination is needed to separate attention, eye-movement, and visual-field deficits.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_superior_colliculus",
      "lb_parinaud"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_tail_of_hippocampus": {
    "functionText": "The hippocampal tail is the posterior segment of the hippocampal formation. Posterior hippocampal networks are often particularly engaged by fine spatial distinctions and detailed contextual representations. These are graded preferences shared with neighboring body regions, not an exclusive navigation function; episodic memory draws on coordinated activity throughout the hippocampus and connected cortex.",
    "connectionsText": "Posterior hippocampal regions interact strongly with parahippocampal, retrosplenial, and posterior medial cortical networks involved in context and spatial orientation. Intrinsic long-axis connections integrate this information with more anterior representations. Fibers collect into the fimbria and fornix, while entorhinal and subicular routes exchange information with surrounding medial temporal and distributed cortical systems.",
    "clinicalText": "Posterior hippocampal injury may contribute to impaired detailed spatial or contextual memory, but isolated tail lesions are uncommon and effects depend on wider damage. Long-axis anatomy also changes with development and aging. A small or asymmetric tail is not itself proof of a navigation deficit, epilepsy, or neurodegeneration; clinical and longitudinal evidence are needed.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_hippocampus_gradients",
      "lb_hippocampus_development",
      "lb_hippocampus_molecular"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_tegmentum_of_medulla_oblongata": {
    "functionText": "The medullary tegmentum contains networks for visceral regulation, swallowing, sensation, and cranial motor functions together with ascending and descending pathways. It contributes to breathing and cardiovascular control through multiple distributed nuclei, not a single vital center. Its broad extent means that medial, lateral, dorsal, and ventrolateral injuries can produce very different combinations of deficits.",
    "connectionsText": "The solitary tract nucleus receives visceral sensory information, including vagal and glossopharyngeal inputs, while nucleus ambiguus and other motor nuclei influence swallowing and phonation. Reticular circuits connect with respiratory and autonomic systems; sensory pathways continue toward thalamus, and cerebellar routes convey coordination signals. These named components are neighboring and interacting systems within the larger tegmental territory.",
    "clinicalText": "Lateral medullary injury can combine swallowing difficulty, hoarseness, vertigo, ipsilateral facial sensory changes, and contralateral body pain-temperature loss. Other medullary locations produce different patterns, and extensive involvement may threaten respiration or cardiovascular stability. The atlas region alone cannot predict severity; localization requires identifying which nuclei, tracts, and sides are actually affected.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_medulla",
      "lb_brainstem"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_third_ventricle": {
    "functionText": "The third ventricle is the narrow midline CSF space within the diencephalon. Its choroid plexus contributes fluid, and the cavity connects the paired lateral ventricles with the aqueduct. It is not a thalamic or hypothalamic processing center: sensory relay, endocrine regulation, and homeostasis are functions of the neural tissues forming its walls.",
    "connectionsText": "The interventricular foramina of Monro open into its anterior region, and the cerebral aqueduct leads posteriorly toward the fourth ventricle. Thalamic and hypothalamic structures form its lateral walls, with optic and infundibular recesses associated with its floor. These are fluid communications and anatomical borders, not axonal pathways between the cavity and adjacent nuclei.",
    "clinicalText": "A colloid cyst near Monro can obstruct lateral ventricular drainage, while aqueductal blockage can enlarge the third and lateral ventricles together. Lesions around the floor may also affect hypothalamic or optic structures. Ventricular shape therefore helps localize an obstruction, but symptoms and urgency depend on pressure, onset, and adjacent tissue involvement rather than size alone.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_ventricles_neuroscience",
      "lb_ventricles",
      "lb_csf_disorders"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_ventral_posterior_lateral_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The ventral posterior lateral nucleus, or VPL, is a major thalamic relay for somatic sensation from the body. It contributes to discriminative touch, proprioceptive, and pain-related processing before cortical interpretation. Its organized body representation contrasts with the neighboring VPM’s emphasis on the face, although sensation ultimately depends on several interacting thalamic and cortical pathways.",
    "connectionsText": "The medial lemniscus supplies information about body touch, vibration, and position after crossing in the medulla; spinothalamic pathways contribute pain and temperature-related signals. VPL neurons project through thalamocortical fibers toward the body representation in somatosensory cortex. Cortical feedback and thalamic inhibitory circuits regulate this traffic rather than leaving the relay fixed or purely feedforward.",
    "clinicalText": "VPL injury can produce contralateral body sensory loss or altered sensation, sometimes with relatively preserved facial sensation when VPM is spared. Damage involving the posterior sensory thalamus can also contribute to central post-stroke pain, but pain does not occur after every lesion. Findings depend on vascular territory and whether adjacent nuclei or internal-capsule fibers are involved.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_thalamic_nuclei",
      "lb_thalamic_pain",
      "lb_thalamic_radiations"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_ventral_posterior_medial_nucleus": {
    "functionText": "The ventral posterior medial nucleus, or VPM, is an important relay for facial and oral somatic sensation. It helps convey touch and other sensory information toward the cortical face representation. A specialized parvicellular territory is associated with taste pathways, so the broad VPM label contains functionally distinct components rather than one uniform facial-sensation channel.",
    "connectionsText": "Ascending trigeminal pathways bring information from principal and spinal trigeminal nuclei to VPM, with major projections representing the opposite face. Thalamocortical fibers then reach somatosensory cortex. Taste-related parvicellular pathways connect with gustatory cortical regions. These routes should be distinguished from neighboring VPL body-sensation pathways and from brainstem circuits that mediate facial reflexes directly.",
    "clinicalText": "A small VPM-region lesion may cause contralateral facial or oral numbness, sometimes with adjacent hand involvement when neighboring sensory representations are affected. Broader posterior thalamic injury can produce unpleasant or painful sensory disturbances. Facial weakness alone instead suggests motor pathway involvement, so sensory examination and lesion extent are necessary to interpret this nucleus’s clinical relevance.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_thalamic_nuclei",
      "lb_trigeminal",
      "lb_thalamic_pain"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_white_matter_of_forebrain": {
    "functionText": "Forebrain white matter is the broad collection of axons and supporting glia connecting cerebral regions with one another and with deeper structures. Association fibers connect regions within a hemisphere, commissural fibers connect hemispheres, and projection fibers link cortex with subcortical targets. This large atlas envelope is not a single tract or a uniform functional processing center.",
    "connectionsText": "Examples include corpus callosum and anterior commissure, long association bundles, corona radiata, and internal-capsule pathways. These systems connect frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital cortex with each other and with thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem, and spinal networks. A broad white matter mesh cannot specify which fibers pass through any selected point; named tracts provide more precise context.",
    "clinicalText": "White matter injury can disconnect functioning gray-matter regions, with deficits determined by tract location and lesion extent. Stroke, demyelination, traumatic axonal injury, and other processes may cause different patterns of weakness, sensory loss, language difficulty, or slowed cognition. An atlas envelope or nonspecific imaging signal does not identify the underlying cause or establish a single syndrome.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_internal_capsule",
      "lb_commissures",
      "lb_thalamic_radiations"
    ]
  },
  "Allen_white_matter_of_hindbrain": {
    "functionText": "Hindbrain white matter comprises many axonal pathways within the pons, medulla, and cerebellar region. These fibers carry motor, sensory, vestibular, autonomic-related, and cerebellar information between separated neural populations. The label is a regional tissue grouping rather than one functional bundle, and it should be distinguished from the nuclei embedded among or adjacent to these pathways.",
    "connectionsText": "Examples include corticospinal fibers, ascending sensory tracts, transverse pontocerebellar fibers, cerebellar peduncles, and cerebellar white matter connecting cortex with deep nuclei. Some routes cross the midline, while others remain on one side or participate in bilateral circuits. Identifying the named tract and its crossing level is more informative than assigning one direction to all hindbrain white matter.",
    "clinicalText": "A small hindbrain white matter lesion can disrupt several tightly packed pathways, producing combinations of weakness, sensory change, ataxia, or eye-movement disturbance. Vascular, demyelinating, degenerative, and other causes have different distributions. Laterality depends on where each tract crosses, and symptoms also change when nearby cranial nerve or reticular nuclei are involved.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_brainstem",
      "lb_middle_peduncle",
      "lb_pyramidal_lesions",
      "lb_cerebellum"
    ]
  },
  "VH_M_optic_chiasm": {
    "functionText": "The optic chiasm is the partial crossing of retinal axons at the base of the forebrain. Fibers from each nasal retina cross while temporal-retinal fibers largely remain on the same side. This rearrangement places information about each visual hemifield into the opposite optic tract; the chiasm routes signals rather than interpreting visual scenes itself.",
    "connectionsText": "The optic nerves enter anteriorly and the optic tracts leave posteriorly. Nasal-retinal fibers carry temporal visual-field information, explaining the field pattern produced by central chiasmal damage. The chiasm lies above the pituitary region and near hypothalamic structures; those are anatomical neighbors, not evidence that the chiasm regulates hormones or performs hypothalamic functions.",
    "clinicalText": "Compression from a sellar or suprasellar mass can preferentially damage crossing fibers and produce bitemporal visual-field loss. The actual pattern varies with chiasmal position and lesion extent, and asymmetric or junctional findings can occur. Visual acuity may remain relatively preserved early, making formal field testing important when evaluating a suspected chiasmal process.",
    "sourceIds": [
      "lb_optic_chiasm",
      "lb_visual_pathway"
    ]
  }
};
